1
00:00:03,772 --> 00:00:10,212
♪ In the not-too-distant
future--next Sunday, A.D.-- ♪

2
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:12,613
♪ There was a guy named Joel ♪

3
00:00:12,647 --> 00:00:16,050
♪ Not too different
from you or me ♪

4
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:19,453
♪ He worked
at Gizmonic Institute ♪

5
00:00:19,487 --> 00:00:22,557
♪ Just another face
in a red jumpsuit ♪

6
00:00:22,590 --> 00:00:25,560
♪ He did a good job cleaning up
the place ♪

7
00:00:25,593 --> 00:00:27,328
♪ But his bosses
didn't like him ♪

8
00:00:27,362 --> 00:00:31,698
♪ So they shot him into space ♪

9
00:00:31,732 --> 00:00:34,702
♪ We'll send him cheesy movies ♪

10
00:00:34,735 --> 00:00:36,537
♪ The worst we can find ♪

11
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:38,172
♪ La-la-la ♪

12
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:41,108
♪ He'll have to sit
and watch them all ♪

13
00:00:41,142 --> 00:00:44,245
♪ Then we'll monitor his mind ♪

14
00:00:44,278 --> 00:00:46,815
♪ Now keep in mind
Joel can't control ♪

15
00:00:46,848 --> 00:00:50,418
♪ When the movies begin or end ♪

16
00:00:50,452 --> 00:00:53,220
♪ Because he used
those special parts ♪

17
00:00:53,253 --> 00:00:58,225
♪ To make his robot friends ♪

18
00:00:58,258 --> 00:00:59,693
♪ Robot roll-call-- ♪

19
00:00:59,727 --> 00:01:01,495
♪ Cambot ♪

20
00:01:01,528 --> 00:01:02,630
♪ Gypsy ♪

21
00:01:02,663 --> 00:01:04,165
♪ Tom Servo ♪

22
00:01:04,198 --> 00:01:05,533
♪ Crow ♪

23
00:01:05,567 --> 00:01:09,470
♪ If you're wondering
how he eats and breathes ♪

24
00:01:09,503 --> 00:01:11,638
♪ And other science facts ♪

25
00:01:11,672 --> 00:01:13,608
- ♪ La-la-la ♪
- ♪ Then repeat to yourself ♪

26
00:01:13,642 --> 00:01:18,445
♪ "It's just a show,
I should really just relax ♪

27
00:01:18,478 --> 00:01:24,385
♪ For Mystery Science
Theater 3000." ♪

28
00:01:34,628 --> 00:01:39,300
- Ow. Ow.
- There. All done, okay.

29
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:41,602
Hi, everybody.
I'm just giving Tom Servo

30
00:01:41,635 --> 00:01:44,572
the equivalent
of a robot haircut.

31
00:01:44,605 --> 00:01:47,642
You're next. Have a seat.
Just kidding.

32
00:01:47,675 --> 00:01:49,810
- 30 seconds to commercial sign.

33
00:01:49,843 --> 00:01:51,712
- What do you think, Tom?

34
00:01:51,745 --> 00:01:54,415
- Hey, very sporty!

35
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:56,550
- Hey, you kind of look like
that guy from House Party.

36
00:01:56,583 --> 00:01:57,552
I think that's kind of neat.

37
00:01:57,585 --> 00:01:59,453
- Joel.
- There you are, Crow.

38
00:01:59,487 --> 00:02:00,888
- Great. Now to keep it
looking good,

39
00:02:00,922 --> 00:02:02,757
you're going to need some
of these accoutrements.

40
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:04,692
There's the styling gel.

41
00:02:04,725 --> 00:02:07,262
There's the styling gel
activator rub.

42
00:02:07,295 --> 00:02:09,263
That comes
after the styling gel.

43
00:02:09,297 --> 00:02:11,232
There's the vapor mist hood.

44
00:02:11,265 --> 00:02:14,668
That comes after the styling
mist activator gel.

45
00:02:14,702 --> 00:02:17,238
- Or before I need styling.
- Right. And then there's

46
00:02:17,272 --> 00:02:21,375
the pre-shampoo--
No, this is the shampoo.

47
00:02:21,408 --> 00:02:25,747
- Commercial sign in five,
four, three, two--

48
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,248
- Commercial sign now.
- We'll be right back.

49
00:02:28,282 --> 00:02:31,552
- You know, teasing plastic
takes protein out.

50
00:02:31,585 --> 00:02:35,423
Using polycarbon 21 religiously.
We'll be right back.

51
00:02:41,362 --> 00:02:43,698
- Oh, you're back. Boy.

52
00:02:43,732 --> 00:02:46,567
Step down, grab a lollipop.
Good job there.

53
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:48,803
I guess it's time
for the invention exchange,

54
00:02:48,837 --> 00:02:49,903
if I'm not mistaken.

55
00:02:49,937 --> 00:02:52,807
- Look, I'll say when it is
and when it is not time

56
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:53,973
for the invention exchange.

57
00:02:54,007 --> 00:02:57,945
And I say that it is time
for the invention exchange.

58
00:02:57,978 --> 00:03:00,448
Isn't that right, Frank?

59
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:03,685
- Duck was playing like that.

60
00:03:03,718 --> 00:03:08,322
- Look, our invention is rather
complex this week, Joel.

61
00:03:08,355 --> 00:03:09,923
So why don't you go first.

62
00:03:09,957 --> 00:03:11,492
- Well, I was going to show you

63
00:03:11,525 --> 00:03:13,795
the Mexican Jumping
Bean Bag Chair this week.

64
00:03:13,828 --> 00:03:17,598
It was really great
for rearranging the house.

65
00:03:17,632 --> 00:03:21,802
Excellent for the elderly
circulation.

66
00:03:21,835 --> 00:03:24,638
And if the vinyl ripped,
it would cover itself

67
00:03:24,672 --> 00:03:28,710
in a tangy cheese sauce.
But again, Gypsy ate it.

68
00:03:28,743 --> 00:03:31,578
- She's got Montezuma's revenge
like you wouldn't believe.

69
00:03:31,612 --> 00:03:33,915
- It's too bad.
So I had to pull this one out.

70
00:03:33,948 --> 00:03:34,815
It's not really done.

71
00:03:34,848 --> 00:03:36,583
But I think the concept's
kinda neat.

72
00:03:36,617 --> 00:03:38,753
You know,
preparing taxes on Earth

73
00:03:38,787 --> 00:03:41,588
is a really stressful time
for everybody involved,

74
00:03:41,622 --> 00:03:44,725
especially the person
who has to prepare your taxes.

75
00:03:44,758 --> 00:03:46,693
So I invented
this adding machine

76
00:03:46,727 --> 00:03:49,563
that actually
when it prints out,

77
00:03:49,597 --> 00:03:55,002
it prints out in these kind of
candies that are on paper.

78
00:03:55,035 --> 00:03:58,338
See, that way you can eat
your losses if things go bad.

79
00:03:58,372 --> 00:04:00,775
My favorite flavor is--
In the Red.

80
00:04:00,808 --> 00:04:03,678
- Yeah, turns a bean counter
into a jellybean counter,

81
00:04:03,712 --> 00:04:04,512
you might say.

82
00:04:04,545 --> 00:04:06,480
- Well, those are great.
I love them.

83
00:04:06,513 --> 00:04:08,983
You know what I really liked,
were those wax whistles

84
00:04:09,017 --> 00:04:10,352
that you'd get at Halloween

85
00:04:10,385 --> 00:04:12,487
and those little yellow
candy corn things.

86
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:13,822
Sometimes I'd just
stick them up my nose.

87
00:04:13,855 --> 00:04:16,390
- Look, no one is interested
in your little trip

88
00:04:16,423 --> 00:04:17,958
down memory lane.

89
00:04:17,992 --> 00:04:19,158
- Well, Joel Franklin would be.

90
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:22,597
- And no one is interested in
some candy adding machine.

91
00:04:22,630 --> 00:04:25,298
- Well, actually the ribbon
was the candy part.

92
00:04:25,332 --> 00:04:29,003
- Would you please tell
Joel and the bots

93
00:04:29,037 --> 00:04:30,438
about our new invention?

94
00:04:30,472 --> 00:04:32,840
- Okay. Well, Joel,
if you're like me,

95
00:04:32,873 --> 00:04:35,977
when I think of the '70s,
I think of one thing.

96
00:04:36,010 --> 00:04:38,713
Foosball! Whoo!

97
00:04:38,747 --> 00:04:42,517
So what we've done is we've
taken the whole foosball concept

98
00:04:42,550 --> 00:04:44,818
and we've caulked this,
added water.

99
00:04:44,852 --> 00:04:48,957
And we've turned it into
water polo. Whoo!

100
00:04:48,990 --> 00:04:51,858
- That's right. To provide hours
of aquatic terror.

101
00:04:51,892 --> 00:04:53,027
Get that, Frank.

102
00:04:53,060 --> 00:04:54,995
With shark attacks,
boat accidents--

103
00:04:55,028 --> 00:04:56,530
- I am going to kill you.

104
00:04:56,563 --> 00:04:58,933
- Just like the kind of terror
you're going to see

105
00:04:58,967 --> 00:05:00,802
in this film this week, Joel.

106
00:05:00,835 --> 00:05:02,937
The Phantom Creeps
with Bela Lugosi.

107
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:06,907
- And then after that,
our main feature is called

108
00:05:06,940 --> 00:05:08,575
Rocket Attack U.S.A.

109
00:05:08,608 --> 00:05:10,378
It's the feel good film
of the Cold War era.

110
00:05:10,412 --> 00:05:13,815
A triumph of the human spirit.
But now I'm going to kill you.

111
00:05:13,848 --> 00:05:16,817
- I have you now!
- Die. Die!

112
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:19,620
- I just scored on you!

113
00:05:19,653 --> 00:05:20,553
- Aah!

114
00:05:20,587 --> 00:05:24,592
- Endure the film, Joel.
- I will kill you!

115
00:05:24,625 --> 00:05:27,895
[Alarm sounding, screaming]

116
00:05:39,873 --> 00:05:42,977
- Whoa. Did you see
that foosball thing they did?

117
00:05:43,010 --> 00:05:44,445
- Cool.

118
00:05:44,478 --> 00:05:48,048
- Now is creeps a verb
or a noun in this?

119
00:05:48,082 --> 00:05:50,618
- I think it's a French pancake.

120
00:05:52,587 --> 00:05:55,423
Spotlight dance
on the phantom creep.

121
00:05:55,457 --> 00:05:57,425
Hey, Basel Dick
and Mildred Barish.

122
00:05:57,458 --> 00:06:00,160
I've had that before.

123
00:06:00,193 --> 00:06:04,065
Shopper always leaves you
laughing, ha ha ha ha ha.

124
00:06:04,098 --> 00:06:06,833
- Ford Beebe!
- Ford Beebe!

125
00:06:06,867 --> 00:06:11,172
- What kind of guy?
Oh, it's the good kind. Okay.

126
00:06:11,205 --> 00:06:13,875
- It's a view out of the window
of an HO train there.

127
00:06:13,908 --> 00:06:15,610
- [Imitates Lugosi]
This is my skull.

128
00:06:15,643 --> 00:06:18,847
Stick around an found out
how that could've happen.

129
00:06:18,880 --> 00:06:22,517
I was going to use this skull,
but I welded a phone to it.

130
00:06:22,550 --> 00:06:24,018
Wasn't that puckish of me?

131
00:06:24,052 --> 00:06:26,153
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Nothing's wrong with her skull

132
00:06:26,187 --> 00:06:28,488
that $100 wouldn't fix.

133
00:06:28,522 --> 00:06:30,023
These two, don't get me started.

134
00:06:30,057 --> 00:06:34,162
I just fused their pelvises
together. Oh, boy.

135
00:06:34,195 --> 00:06:36,897
- [Imitates Lugosi] This was
my most fiendish experiment.

136
00:06:36,930 --> 00:06:39,900
I connected these two
by the frame. Very expensive.

137
00:06:39,933 --> 00:06:42,035
- [Imitates Lugosi]
This is my mailing list.

138
00:06:42,068 --> 00:06:44,005
If you're interested,
I'm selling it.

139
00:06:44,038 --> 00:06:45,707
Notice the perfect demographic.

140
00:06:45,740 --> 00:06:49,043
Now back to my HO train--
the Transylvania Choo-Choo.

141
00:06:49,077 --> 00:06:50,177
Pardon me, boy.

142
00:06:50,210 --> 00:06:54,182
In Xanadu did Kubla Khan
a stately pleasure dome decree.

143
00:06:54,215 --> 00:06:58,452
- Forward, I get that's
how you're supposed to read it.

144
00:07:05,225 --> 00:07:08,528
- Mallory?
- Yes, Alex?

145
00:07:13,267 --> 00:07:17,172
- You guys still sure that Lucas
was the first one to do this?

146
00:07:17,205 --> 00:07:20,073
- And so Peter saw the wolf
coming through the woods

147
00:07:20,107 --> 00:07:21,808
followed by an oboe.

148
00:07:21,842 --> 00:07:24,045
- Wait. Who's Jim Daly?
Back that up, will you?

149
00:07:24,078 --> 00:07:25,947
- Gene Gene the dancing machine?

150
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:29,917
- Art Art--
well, you know the rest--

151
00:07:29,950 --> 00:07:32,953
- This wasn't in my bag
when I left the house.

152
00:07:32,987 --> 00:07:34,822
- Oh, a klepto, eh?

153
00:07:34,855 --> 00:07:37,192
- That's the same kind of disc
that was found under the plant.

154
00:07:37,225 --> 00:07:39,560
- The one the slider went to.

155
00:07:39,593 --> 00:07:42,197
- Eh, shut up, Grandma,
and eat your peanuts.

156
00:07:42,230 --> 00:07:44,965
- Will you, Jim? I want to turn
it back to the department.

157
00:07:44,998 --> 00:07:47,000
- Are you sure
you didn't find it and take--

158
00:07:47,033 --> 00:07:49,803
- I hope God is his co-pilot.
- Why of course I didn't.

159
00:07:49,837 --> 00:07:50,837
- From where the car was found,

160
00:07:50,870 --> 00:07:53,607
have you any idea of where
Dr. Zorka was going?

161
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:55,242
- None whatever. Aah!
- Oh!

162
00:07:55,275 --> 00:07:59,113
Now how do you feel about
spontaneous human combustion?

163
00:07:59,147 --> 00:08:01,882
- Sorry I blew up like that.

164
00:08:01,915 --> 00:08:04,252
- That can't be healthy.
- Uh-uh.

165
00:08:04,285 --> 00:08:06,853
- The controls are out of order.
- Plot's out of order?

166
00:08:06,887 --> 00:08:09,490
- Bail out?
I'm not dressed for it.

167
00:08:12,092 --> 00:08:13,292
- What are you doing here?

168
00:08:13,325 --> 00:08:16,097
- I didn't think you'd mind
if I took the last parachute.

169
00:08:16,130 --> 00:08:17,698
You're a dear, thanks.

170
00:08:24,072 --> 00:08:27,140
- Oh, shoots!
- Oh, Professor Firefly,

171
00:08:27,173 --> 00:08:30,812
- it's not my size.
- Stop, drop, and roll.

172
00:08:32,145 --> 00:08:34,282
- Yee-hoo.

173
00:08:34,315 --> 00:08:37,885
Yee-hoo.

174
00:08:41,322 --> 00:08:44,058
- Whoa. Whoa.

175
00:08:46,793 --> 00:08:51,132
- And that's why I'll never fly.
- I got you.

176
00:08:51,165 --> 00:08:53,533
- I can see my house from here.

177
00:08:54,602 --> 00:08:56,837
- Flaming Goose down.

178
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:58,138
- Mr. Zorka.
- Sucker?

179
00:08:58,172 --> 00:09:02,343
She got on the plane,
didn't she? Man.

180
00:09:02,377 --> 00:09:06,013
- Hey, try and move her spine
around as much as possible.

181
00:09:06,047 --> 00:09:08,782
- Weird idea of foreplay,
but when in Rome.

182
00:09:08,815 --> 00:09:11,718
- Wow, I'm gonna get my another
one. This is my lucky day.

183
00:09:11,752 --> 00:09:15,623
- [Imitating barking]

184
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:20,293
- [Imitates Lugosi]
How fortunate,

185
00:09:20,327 --> 00:09:22,763
this will simplify everything.

186
00:09:28,368 --> 00:09:31,672
- Cathy Rigby is Peter Pan.

187
00:09:34,075 --> 00:09:36,343
- Anything you say, Boss.
- Oh, I can see the strings.

188
00:09:36,377 --> 00:09:38,112
Look at that.

189
00:09:38,145 --> 00:09:41,082
- Hmm. Fresh, delish,
tree-ripened girls.

190
00:09:41,115 --> 00:09:42,783
- [Imitates Lugosi]
She's wearing easy spirit

191
00:09:42,817 --> 00:09:44,252
shoes for jumping.

192
00:09:44,285 --> 00:09:46,753
- Just think of her as a pinata.

193
00:09:46,787 --> 00:09:49,223
- Hey, watch the hands.
Come on, hey! Ouch.

194
00:09:49,257 --> 00:09:51,692
That does hurt. Ooh.

195
00:09:51,725 --> 00:09:53,960
- Who was in it?
- Was a government plane.

196
00:09:53,993 --> 00:09:57,097
Two men and another woman.
- And a baby.

197
00:09:57,130 --> 00:10:00,200
- I'm a reporter.
I have to get to that wreck.

198
00:10:00,233 --> 00:10:01,067
- Come on.

199
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:03,770
- I'm a doctor.
I will take you there.

200
00:10:03,803 --> 00:10:06,707
- We sure do have
a lot of adventures, Boss.

201
00:10:08,142 --> 00:10:13,180
Luckily, the policemen's picnic
was just over the hill.

202
00:10:13,213 --> 00:10:16,217
- Hey, did you guys hear the one
about the traveling salesman.

203
00:10:16,250 --> 00:10:19,220
- Okay, buddy, there's
an ambulance on the way.

204
00:10:19,253 --> 00:10:22,857
- But I feel fine.

205
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:25,358
- Thanks for buying
those raffle tickets.

206
00:10:25,392 --> 00:10:27,395
Let's slack off for some donuts.

207
00:10:27,428 --> 00:10:30,732
- I want to get my pen
out of this guy's throat.

208
00:10:30,765 --> 00:10:33,000
I need it back.

209
00:10:38,838 --> 00:10:40,708
- Wait a minute.
- I'm a physician.

210
00:10:40,742 --> 00:10:42,943
- Yeah, Klaus Von Bulo.

211
00:10:42,977 --> 00:10:45,880
- [Imitates Lugosi] Oh,
this simplifies everything.

212
00:10:45,913 --> 00:10:49,283
And to think I was going
to identify her. What a hoot.

213
00:10:49,317 --> 00:10:54,222
She is dead. How unfortunate.
Oh, the irony.

214
00:10:54,255 --> 00:10:59,260
Wait. Wait a minute.
I'm kissing my own hand.

215
00:11:02,328 --> 00:11:05,700
- Hey, I got dibs, fella.

216
00:11:09,303 --> 00:11:11,738
- Well, that's that.

217
00:11:14,007 --> 00:11:16,943
- [Imitates Lugosi]
He's got gum. How fortunate.

218
00:11:16,977 --> 00:11:18,845
- Capt. West,
my people would like

219
00:11:18,878 --> 00:11:20,047
an official statement from you.

220
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:22,182
- Get bent. Is that official
enough for you?

221
00:11:22,215 --> 00:11:23,917
- ...following you
to get a story.

222
00:11:23,950 --> 00:11:25,050
- What do you make of him,
Doctor?

223
00:11:25,083 --> 00:11:26,453
- [Imitates Lugosi] Well,
I could make a crown roast

224
00:11:26,487 --> 00:11:28,288
with ribs all right.

225
00:11:29,857 --> 00:11:31,192
- The man seemed to be
in a coma.

226
00:11:31,225 --> 00:11:33,360
- He watched the first reel
of the film.

227
00:11:33,393 --> 00:11:37,130
- The ambulance is coming.
Take this girl away.

228
00:11:37,163 --> 00:11:38,732
- Come on, lady.

229
00:11:38,765 --> 00:11:40,000
- Capt. West,
I want another connection

230
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:41,902
between this plane crash
and Dr. Zorka's death.

231
00:11:41,935 --> 00:11:44,172
- And unless you tell me.
- All right, arrest her.

232
00:11:44,205 --> 00:11:45,873
- Leave go of me.
- You mean "let go."

233
00:11:45,907 --> 00:11:47,140
- You can't arrest me
without a charge.

234
00:11:47,173 --> 00:11:49,210
- She was stowing away
in a government plane.

235
00:11:49,243 --> 00:11:50,443
- And bad syntax.

236
00:11:50,477 --> 00:11:53,813
- And I'll tell you another
thing too--Hey, wait a minute.

237
00:11:53,847 --> 00:11:57,150
And Jerry Mathers as The Beaver.
Heh heh.

238
00:11:57,183 --> 00:11:59,253
- A thorough examination
of your file...

239
00:11:59,287 --> 00:12:00,520
- And your colon.

240
00:12:00,553 --> 00:12:02,923
- ...convinces me that he is in
a state of suspended animation.

241
00:12:02,957 --> 00:12:05,292
However, I've made arrangements
to have him brought here

242
00:12:05,325 --> 00:12:07,093
- for further observation.
- Of his colon.

243
00:12:07,127 --> 00:12:08,428
- I've been wondering, Doctor...

244
00:12:08,462 --> 00:12:09,963
- Looks like the coffee's done.

245
00:12:09,997 --> 00:12:13,900
- His spine was in perfect shape
when I examined him.

246
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:16,170
- ...plane has disappeared.

247
00:12:16,203 --> 00:12:19,873
- But you saw no spiders.
And Dr. Zorka is dead.

248
00:12:19,907 --> 00:12:21,175
- Yes,
but his secrets aren't dead.

249
00:12:21,208 --> 00:12:23,277
- But his colon is.
- ...sold his formula

250
00:12:23,310 --> 00:12:25,412
to those foreign agents
who were after him.

251
00:12:25,445 --> 00:12:27,347
- That still wouldn't explain
the presence

252
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:28,815
of the element on the plane.

253
00:12:28,848 --> 00:12:30,183
- Or my presence in this film.

254
00:12:30,217 --> 00:12:32,920
- ...and Mrs. Zorka
and yourself aboard.

255
00:12:32,953 --> 00:12:35,122
- As it happens,
there was someone else aboard.

256
00:12:35,155 --> 00:12:40,862
- Or just a colon.
Mystery guest, sign in please.

257
00:12:40,895 --> 00:12:42,963
- Kitty Carlisle.

258
00:12:42,997 --> 00:12:45,198
- Dr. Mallory.
- How do you do?

259
00:12:45,232 --> 00:12:46,398
- The doctor and I
are old friends.

260
00:12:46,432 --> 00:12:48,835
- Oh, so that's how it is.
- Did you check my credentials?

261
00:12:48,868 --> 00:12:50,003
- Yes.
- Satisfied?

262
00:12:50,037 --> 00:12:52,272
- No. We have reason to believe

263
00:12:52,305 --> 00:12:54,242
that something was smuggled
aboard that plane.

264
00:12:54,275 --> 00:12:56,543
- We'll have to do
a cavity search of the plane.

265
00:12:56,577 --> 00:12:58,345
- ...you can't print anything
except what I tell you.

266
00:12:58,378 --> 00:12:59,580
Is that clear?

267
00:12:59,613 --> 00:13:01,982
- Oh, a police state.
- You win. Sure.

268
00:13:03,017 --> 00:13:05,218
- The airplane crash
was a sequel

269
00:13:05,252 --> 00:13:07,420
to the violent death
of Dr. Zorka.

270
00:13:07,453 --> 00:13:09,257
- Staring George Kennedy.

271
00:13:09,290 --> 00:13:12,560
- Paris this year sets news
print, news print, news print.

272
00:13:12,593 --> 00:13:14,093
Yards of it.

273
00:13:15,328 --> 00:13:18,498
- Hmm. Zack Norman is Sammy
in Chief Zaboo.

274
00:13:18,532 --> 00:13:20,033
What do you make of that?

275
00:13:20,067 --> 00:13:24,805
- Extra, extra. Fire hydrant
attacked by newspaper.

276
00:13:26,473 --> 00:13:28,575
- It's rare that a human
interest story makes it

277
00:13:28,608 --> 00:13:29,708
on the front page like this.

278
00:13:29,742 --> 00:13:33,813
- Coincidence? Read the book.
- Okay, we read this.

279
00:13:35,950 --> 00:13:38,885
- Extra! Extra!
- Extra?

280
00:13:42,322 --> 00:13:45,025
Hey, Boss,
says here you're dead.

281
00:13:45,058 --> 00:13:47,562
- [As Lugosi] Rumors of my death
are greatly exaggerated.

282
00:13:47,595 --> 00:13:50,965
Oh, jumbo. Oh, I love it. Heh.

283
00:13:50,998 --> 00:13:52,567
You did the crosswords,
you fool.

284
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:56,970
And look, Tom and Roseanne beat
me out in the press again.

285
00:13:57,003 --> 00:13:59,005
- She's really dead.

286
00:13:59,038 --> 00:14:01,642
- You knew that, Doctor.
You saw her.

287
00:14:01,675 --> 00:14:02,842
- Mallory.

288
00:14:02,875 --> 00:14:06,613
And all the so-called scientists
who drove me into hiding,

289
00:14:06,647 --> 00:14:09,550
they are responsible
for her death.

290
00:14:09,583 --> 00:14:12,352
They shall pay.

291
00:14:12,385 --> 00:14:16,057
- That's my boss. Always going
on about his life of danger.

292
00:14:16,090 --> 00:14:17,490
But look who does
all the driving.

293
00:14:17,523 --> 00:14:18,590
We'll be right back.

294
00:14:18,623 --> 00:14:24,197
- Meanwhile, in Frank Lloyd
Wright building nearby.

295
00:14:24,230 --> 00:14:25,967
- Send this message in code

296
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:27,968
to all out allied foreign
operators.

297
00:14:28,002 --> 00:14:29,437
- Bl-bl-bl-bl-bl!

298
00:14:29,470 --> 00:14:31,372
- As disguise
for our spying operations...

299
00:14:31,405 --> 00:14:34,508
- Bungee jumping--
sport of fools.

300
00:14:34,542 --> 00:14:37,612
- Hey, whoa! Ah.

301
00:14:37,645 --> 00:14:40,013
- Whoa. Wait a minute.

302
00:14:40,047 --> 00:14:42,683
- Oh, they got a little montage!
Go!

303
00:14:42,717 --> 00:14:45,318
- Aah!

304
00:14:53,127 --> 00:14:55,162
- Well, did you get
Zorka's formula?

305
00:14:55,195 --> 00:14:57,597
- Yes, it was juice, toast,
milk, and Trix.

306
00:14:57,630 --> 00:15:02,135
- You've got to get in
or find Zorka's assistant monk.

307
00:15:02,168 --> 00:15:03,970
I'm about to broadcast
a recording

308
00:15:04,003 --> 00:15:06,373
to our friends in Europe,
informing them of Zorka's death.

309
00:15:06,407 --> 00:15:09,410
All right, I'll go myself.
I'll assemble the men tonight.

310
00:15:09,443 --> 00:15:11,412
We may be able to slip past
the guards.

311
00:15:11,445 --> 00:15:13,513
- Think I could have
some lunch money?

312
00:15:13,547 --> 00:15:16,417
- Get back to your letter,
you wiener.

313
00:15:16,450 --> 00:15:18,685
I'll be in my office.
Oh, I don't have one.

314
00:15:18,718 --> 00:15:20,955
See you later, not.

315
00:15:22,455 --> 00:15:25,358
- Kill him.

316
00:15:25,392 --> 00:15:26,560
- I'm about to broadcast
exercise 47

317
00:15:26,593 --> 00:15:28,362
with code interruptions.

318
00:15:28,395 --> 00:15:30,063
- All right, begin.

319
00:15:30,097 --> 00:15:31,398
- Well, the old clock
on the wall

320
00:15:31,432 --> 00:15:34,067
says it's time
for the Longene Symphonette.

321
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:35,100
[Telegraph taps]

322
00:15:35,133 --> 00:15:40,573
- ...was...killed...in...
automobile accident.

323
00:15:40,607 --> 00:15:42,142
- Yes, it's Flipper McGee
and Molly

324
00:15:42,175 --> 00:15:43,643
and international Morse code.

325
00:15:43,677 --> 00:15:46,580
- Don't open that closet.

326
00:15:46,613 --> 00:15:50,383
- Inform his majesty at once.

327
00:15:50,417 --> 00:15:53,287
- That for Jim?
- It's for me.

328
00:15:53,320 --> 00:15:55,055
- No.
But it may well revive him.

329
00:15:55,088 --> 00:15:56,423
- It'll sure hurt him.

330
00:15:56,457 --> 00:15:58,592
- This is an antidote
that I prepared in anticipation

331
00:15:58,625 --> 00:15:59,727
of such an event as this.

332
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:01,462
- It's an amusing little
anecdote.

333
00:16:01,495 --> 00:16:03,463
- This is the first opportunity
I've had to test the antidote.

334
00:16:03,497 --> 00:16:06,667
- Usually I just get
the puppy drunk.

335
00:16:06,700 --> 00:16:09,302
- I will never love again.

336
00:16:09,335 --> 00:16:11,405
- No, something is missing.
Some essential ingredient

337
00:16:11,438 --> 00:16:13,607
that I've failed to hit upon.

338
00:16:13,640 --> 00:16:14,607
- How do you suppose to find it?

339
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:16,643
- Make another search
of Zorka's laboratory.

340
00:16:16,677 --> 00:16:18,478
- And this time wear clothing.

341
00:16:18,512 --> 00:16:19,678
- ...someone else
beats us to it.

342
00:16:19,712 --> 00:16:21,148
- Oh, like everybody else
in the world is gonna be there.

343
00:16:21,182 --> 00:16:23,583
- Meanwhile
at the Addams' house.

344
00:16:25,585 --> 00:16:28,022
- Is that Raul Julia?

345
00:16:28,055 --> 00:16:30,457
- Did you hear anything yet,
Mack?

346
00:16:30,490 --> 00:16:34,628
- No.
- Nah!

347
00:16:34,662 --> 00:16:36,097
- The place is guarded.

348
00:16:36,130 --> 00:16:37,732
I can't reach
the secret entrance.

349
00:16:37,765 --> 00:16:39,332
[Animal noises]

350
00:16:39,365 --> 00:16:42,635
- It's Wheeler and Woolsey.
- What's that?

351
00:16:42,668 --> 00:16:43,803
- We have a guest.

352
00:16:43,837 --> 00:16:47,607
- Would you put your hands down?

353
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:52,012
- What about the bullets?

354
00:16:53,513 --> 00:16:58,652
- That's my boss, always getting
us into kooky situations.

355
00:16:58,685 --> 00:17:00,520
But I'll be back later.

356
00:17:00,553 --> 00:17:04,492
- You think you got problems?
My bladder's the size of a--

357
00:17:04,525 --> 00:17:06,793
Oh, I'll take a picture of you.
And then I'll...

358
00:17:06,827 --> 00:17:08,495
I quit developing.

359
00:17:08,528 --> 00:17:11,232
- Still shaking the bushes,
Boss.

360
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:16,035
- Halt!

361
00:17:17,537 --> 00:17:19,338
- [Imitates Elmer Fudd]
Be very quiet.

362
00:17:19,372 --> 00:17:22,308
I'm hunting sidekwicks.

363
00:17:22,342 --> 00:17:25,445
- All right, come on.
Back to the house.

364
00:17:25,478 --> 00:17:27,213
Ugh.

365
00:17:27,247 --> 00:17:29,517
- Does this mean we're not going
back to the house?

366
00:17:31,518 --> 00:17:35,522
- You had me worried, Boss.
That was too close for comfort.

367
00:17:35,555 --> 00:17:39,627
- Would you please turn off
The Little Rascals music?

368
00:17:43,730 --> 00:17:45,398
[Whistle blows]

369
00:17:49,803 --> 00:17:51,572
- What's wrong?

370
00:17:51,605 --> 00:17:53,640
- I stopped a guy that
was prowling around here.

371
00:17:53,673 --> 00:17:55,775
And then something hit me
from behind, and he got away.

372
00:17:55,808 --> 00:17:59,345
- I love that story.
Now let's get moving.

373
00:17:59,378 --> 00:18:01,648
- Better not tell Capt. West
that kind of a yarn.

374
00:18:01,682 --> 00:18:03,617
- I told you there's something
queer about this place.

375
00:18:03,650 --> 00:18:06,320
- It's Oscar Wilde's birthplace,
so what?

376
00:18:10,523 --> 00:18:13,160
- Draw the curtains.
- What color?

377
00:18:14,460 --> 00:18:16,763
- Set the sound detector.

378
00:18:16,797 --> 00:18:20,367
- Hey, it looks like
a Heathkit electric chair.

379
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:22,302
You don't see those very often.

380
00:18:22,335 --> 00:18:24,638
- Move all the spiders
and discs.

381
00:18:24,672 --> 00:18:26,138
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Disc is disc.

382
00:18:26,172 --> 00:18:28,608
- ...Mallory searches
this place again

383
00:18:28,642 --> 00:18:31,878
Mallory came into possession
of those discs.

384
00:18:31,912 --> 00:18:35,448
- Be careful with my magic show.

385
00:18:35,482 --> 00:18:36,783
- [Imitates Lugosi]
And turn on the thermostat.

386
00:18:36,817 --> 00:18:39,920
It's freezing looking at
my bug collection.

387
00:18:39,953 --> 00:18:41,855
- And antidote from this?

388
00:18:41,888 --> 00:18:45,158
He must be pretty good.

389
00:18:45,192 --> 00:18:48,695
- In science he was second
only to myself.

390
00:18:48,728 --> 00:18:51,698
- Why not put one
of your spiders after him?

391
00:18:51,732 --> 00:18:54,768
- Oh, that's it.
I've had it with you, fat boy.

392
00:18:54,802 --> 00:18:57,303
- His time has not yet come.

393
00:18:57,337 --> 00:18:59,172
- Creepy crawlers.
Creepy crawlers.

394
00:18:59,205 --> 00:19:00,507
Creepy crawlers.
Creepy crawlers.

395
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:01,775
- Tails.
- How can you tell?

396
00:19:01,808 --> 00:19:03,243
- Look out!

397
00:19:03,277 --> 00:19:05,678
- Oh, man!
- Whoops. Excuse me.

398
00:19:05,712 --> 00:19:08,948
Uh, that's
Mr. Idiot to you, Boss.

399
00:19:08,982 --> 00:19:13,253
- One of my little pets
mistake you?

400
00:19:13,287 --> 00:19:16,357
- Gee, next time take
the rest of my head off.

401
00:19:16,390 --> 00:19:19,192
- I'm ready for my mystery date.

402
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:29,903
- I'm all ready.

403
00:19:29,937 --> 00:19:31,272
Get the rope
and go for the door.

404
00:19:31,305 --> 00:19:32,738
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Okay, wait a minute.

405
00:19:32,772 --> 00:19:34,642
I give the orders around here.

406
00:19:39,245 --> 00:19:42,248
- Hello, Daddy.

407
00:19:42,282 --> 00:19:43,617
I was really scared.

408
00:19:43,650 --> 00:19:47,420
I dreamed there was a little kid
in my closet with ookie.

409
00:19:53,593 --> 00:19:55,995
- I'm no horticulturalist.
But I think these need water.

410
00:19:56,028 --> 00:19:57,363
- Huh?

411
00:19:57,397 --> 00:20:00,833
- What's this?
A dark communion wafer?

412
00:20:00,867 --> 00:20:02,468
- Lame.

413
00:20:04,705 --> 00:20:06,205
- Well...

414
00:20:10,777 --> 00:20:12,845
- [Imitates Lugosi]
He's no good for light dusting.

415
00:20:12,878 --> 00:20:15,915
But on heavy jobs,
he's just wonderful.

416
00:20:15,948 --> 00:20:16,848
- There's someone in the house.

417
00:20:16,882 --> 00:20:20,620
- Ooh, babe alert.
Hot Madonna babe alert.

418
00:20:22,722 --> 00:20:28,362
- Uh, there. That's just
to show you I can do it.

419
00:20:28,395 --> 00:20:31,332
Oh, I'm sorry. It's my line.

420
00:20:31,365 --> 00:20:33,800
I'm still working on my block.
Excuse me.

421
00:20:33,833 --> 00:20:36,570
Want to see my Cagney?

422
00:20:36,603 --> 00:20:39,472
- [Mumbles]

423
00:20:39,505 --> 00:20:41,675
- What?
- Huh?

424
00:20:42,642 --> 00:20:45,345
- Uh, check your coat, sir?

425
00:20:45,378 --> 00:20:47,747
- He's a robot.

426
00:20:47,780 --> 00:20:51,552
- [Mumbles]

427
00:20:51,585 --> 00:20:56,357
- Hmm. 1313 Mockingbird Lane.
Doesn't look scary to me.

428
00:20:56,390 --> 00:20:57,723
- Any trouble, Mack?
- Yes, sir.

429
00:20:57,757 --> 00:20:59,860
- Mr. Paller, sir,
we drove him off.

430
00:20:59,893 --> 00:21:03,697
- I must not show my power
until I'm ready to strike.

431
00:21:03,730 --> 00:21:05,998
- [Imitates Lugosi]
We'll take it on the road--

432
00:21:06,032 --> 00:21:08,635
New Haven. Boston. Springfield.

433
00:21:10,770 --> 00:21:15,275
- You know, for a robot
he's got a really flat butt.

434
00:21:20,813 --> 00:21:21,913
- See what's out there.

435
00:21:21,947 --> 00:21:23,783
- All right, Captain,
as long as we didn't get inside.

436
00:21:23,817 --> 00:21:26,653
- Well, I got to third
if that counts for anything.

437
00:21:28,455 --> 00:21:30,923
- The secret entrance. Let's go.

438
00:21:30,957 --> 00:21:32,392
- Did you forget the gun?

439
00:21:32,425 --> 00:21:34,795
- Oh, yeah.

440
00:21:34,828 --> 00:21:37,630
Uh, phone company.
Pizza delivery.

441
00:21:37,663 --> 00:21:38,763
Gas company.

442
00:21:38,797 --> 00:21:41,802
- Secret entrance. That looks
like a front door to me.

443
00:21:44,972 --> 00:21:48,742
Landlord, Mr. Zorka.

444
00:21:50,843 --> 00:21:53,647
- Ew.

445
00:21:53,680 --> 00:21:54,347
- Aah!

446
00:21:54,380 --> 00:21:57,350
- Sounds like Bill.
- Aah!

447
00:21:57,383 --> 00:22:00,920
- Bill, we're coming, boy!

448
00:22:00,953 --> 00:22:02,555
- Shoot the spider.

449
00:22:02,588 --> 00:22:05,025
- Oh, be proud.
He shot a spider.

450
00:22:05,058 --> 00:22:07,093
Thank you, John Goodman.

451
00:22:07,127 --> 00:22:10,463
- I'll not be taken.
I'll shoot my way out the front.

452
00:22:10,497 --> 00:22:13,567
- You fool!
- Ah, that'd be me, Mr. Fool.

453
00:22:13,600 --> 00:22:16,870
- Hold it!
- Oh, a Milwaukee cop, huh?

454
00:22:16,903 --> 00:22:20,973
- Aah!
- ♪ Roller coaster of love ♪

455
00:22:21,007 --> 00:22:23,410
- Aah!

456
00:22:24,978 --> 00:22:26,580
- Boy.

457
00:22:26,613 --> 00:22:28,615
- I wonder if being invisible
stops bullets.

458
00:22:28,648 --> 00:22:29,815
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Now that I'm invisible,

459
00:22:29,848 --> 00:22:32,752
I'll secretly switch their
coffee with Folger's crystals.

460
00:22:33,887 --> 00:22:35,388
- Stay en guarde.

461
00:22:35,422 --> 00:22:37,690
[Gunfire]
- Run, run, run!

462
00:22:37,723 --> 00:22:39,358
- Stagger!

463
00:22:46,032 --> 00:22:48,168
- My boss always rigging things

464
00:22:48,202 --> 00:22:49,903
so I'm the clown
that takes the bullets.

465
00:22:49,937 --> 00:22:53,607
- Is he dead?
- No, just stunned.

466
00:22:53,640 --> 00:22:55,442
- Stunned? He took six bullets.

467
00:23:00,513 --> 00:23:02,115
[Engine roars]

468
00:23:02,148 --> 00:23:04,683
- It's Christine.
- Christine!

469
00:23:06,018 --> 00:23:06,852
- Here it goes.

470
00:23:06,885 --> 00:23:08,155
- My car's back there
in the trees.

471
00:23:08,188 --> 00:23:09,957
- How'd it get in the trees?

472
00:23:09,990 --> 00:23:14,593
- He's going to get a DWI--
driving while invisible. Ha ha.

473
00:23:14,627 --> 00:23:18,665
- 1928 Porter.
That's my mother dear.

474
00:23:18,698 --> 00:23:21,502
- Doesn't she help us
through everything.

475
00:23:21,535 --> 00:23:24,938
- I'm so glad she's here.
- Please.

476
00:23:26,840 --> 00:23:31,745
- Hey, guys.
I think that's James Dean there.

477
00:23:31,778 --> 00:23:33,913
Over the side. Over the side.

478
00:23:33,947 --> 00:23:35,848
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Ha ha. Can't touch this.

479
00:23:35,882 --> 00:23:37,850
[Chuckles]

480
00:23:37,883 --> 00:23:39,052
- Meanwhile in another
part of the world

481
00:23:39,085 --> 00:23:40,153
thousands of miles away.

482
00:23:40,187 --> 00:23:41,722
- There it is!

483
00:23:41,755 --> 00:23:43,890
- Not.

484
00:23:43,923 --> 00:23:45,592
- Get it.

485
00:23:48,895 --> 00:23:50,797
- [Imitates Lugosi]
Casper, the friendly ghost.

486
00:23:50,830 --> 00:23:54,067
This is great. I can pick
my nose and sing to the radio,

487
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:55,133
and nobody will see me.

488
00:23:59,038 --> 00:24:02,742
- Now we thought it would be
funny to place a rock

489
00:24:02,775 --> 00:24:04,010
under the tire of the car.

490
00:24:04,043 --> 00:24:07,613
Now here comes Fannie Flagg
to help us.

491
00:24:09,248 --> 00:24:12,052
- Still shaking the bushes,
Boss.

492
00:24:14,020 --> 00:24:15,088
- [Sounds like Reagan]
The driver is gone

493
00:24:15,122 --> 00:24:17,190
or he's hiding. You stay here.

494
00:24:17,223 --> 00:24:19,792
- [Imitates Reagan]
Welcome to Death Valley Days.

495
00:24:19,825 --> 00:24:22,962
- He's almost as visible
as I was when I was president.

496
00:24:22,995 --> 00:24:24,797
- Oh, mommy.

497
00:24:28,602 --> 00:24:31,070
- No shaking the bushes.

498
00:24:31,103 --> 00:24:33,172
- And get that frog out of here.

499
00:24:33,205 --> 00:24:35,508
- He left a good tip.

500
00:24:39,145 --> 00:24:39,945
- I know it's a wooden nickel,

501
00:24:39,978 --> 00:24:41,782
and you're not supposed
to take 'em.

502
00:24:41,815 --> 00:24:43,583
But he's going to disprove
that myth.

503
00:24:43,617 --> 00:24:46,920
- Oh, what the heck.
Yeah, I'm keeping it.

504
00:24:46,953 --> 00:24:49,655
And I'm taking
this juicy car too.

505
00:24:49,688 --> 00:24:53,058
- Yeah. Still shaking
the bushes, Boss.

506
00:24:53,092 --> 00:24:57,263
- Ha ha. We got to send this
in to Bob Saget.

507
00:24:57,297 --> 00:25:00,633
- It's just priceless.
- Like the old saying goes--

508
00:25:00,667 --> 00:25:04,203
walk invisibly
and carry a big stick.

509
00:25:04,237 --> 00:25:06,505
- Ugh.
- Owie!

510
00:25:09,977 --> 00:25:12,978
- This will win me
the grand prize for sure.

511
00:25:14,747 --> 00:25:18,017
- Ho ho. I kill me.
And others too.

512
00:25:18,050 --> 00:25:20,753
- Hey,
you've got my overnight bag!

513
00:25:20,787 --> 00:25:22,855
- Let's get out of here, Scooby.

514
00:25:22,888 --> 00:25:25,758
- Crashing Timbers? Isn't that
a place in the Bulkhouse?

515
00:25:25,792 --> 00:25:27,027
- All right,
all of you out of here.

516
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:28,293
I got to clean up.

517
00:25:28,327 --> 00:25:31,898
Now unless you're going to give
to the Will Rogers Fund--aah!

518
00:25:43,910 --> 00:25:47,880
- Boy, that phantom sure
did creep...at a slow pace.

519
00:25:47,913 --> 00:25:51,017
Well Rocket Attack USA
should be exciting.

520
00:25:51,050 --> 00:25:52,918
The overlords
said it was a war film.

521
00:25:52,952 --> 00:25:55,055
- Actually,
I think it's a Cold War film.

522
00:25:55,088 --> 00:25:57,823
- Now what the heck was
the Cold War anyway?

523
00:25:57,857 --> 00:26:00,793
- Well, the Cold War was a rich,
fertile time of paranoia,

524
00:26:00,827 --> 00:26:04,130
virulent conservativism,
rampant jingoism.

525
00:26:04,163 --> 00:26:06,165
- Ah, the Reagan era.

526
00:26:06,198 --> 00:26:08,835
- Actually,
I think it was the McCarthy era.

527
00:26:08,868 --> 00:26:10,203
- Oh the Charlie McCarthy era.

528
00:26:10,237 --> 00:26:14,073
- Well, you're right. Exactly.
It was the McCarthy era.

529
00:26:14,107 --> 00:26:16,075
These are some
artist renderings.

530
00:26:16,108 --> 00:26:18,043
You want to bring it in,
Cambot, just a little bit?

531
00:26:18,077 --> 00:26:23,082
You see, I have here
these artist renderings.

532
00:26:23,115 --> 00:26:25,952
You see one day Charlie McCarthy
looked at Howdy Doody's hair

533
00:26:25,985 --> 00:26:30,690
and saw red. After that, Charlie
formed the McCarthy hearings

534
00:26:30,723 --> 00:26:32,625
on un-American activities.

535
00:26:32,658 --> 00:26:36,830
Howdy turned informant
and named names.

536
00:26:36,863 --> 00:26:40,767
Gumby, Pokey, Kukla, and Ollie
were all implicated

537
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:41,967
on Howdy's damning testimony.

538
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:45,038
Turns out Gumby and Pokey
were in bed with the Chinese.

539
00:26:45,072 --> 00:26:48,675
Their arrests started the whole
Free Art Clokey movement.

540
00:26:48,708 --> 00:26:50,977
Kukla, Ollie had worked on
a left-leaning

541
00:26:51,010 --> 00:26:53,045
Clifford Odets'
Playhouse 90 scrift.

542
00:26:53,078 --> 00:26:55,082
But they were cleared
of all charges.

543
00:26:55,115 --> 00:26:57,117
One of the era's
biggest informants--

544
00:26:57,150 --> 00:27:00,853
- Oh, no. Not the duck
from You Bet Your Life.

545
00:27:00,887 --> 00:27:03,122
- He was a good friend of
arch-conservative

546
00:27:03,155 --> 00:27:04,388
Marx brother writer
Moory Riskin.

547
00:27:04,422 --> 00:27:08,762
And as far as he was concerned,
the secret word was subversive.

548
00:27:08,795 --> 00:27:10,763
After that duck's devastating
testimony,

549
00:27:10,797 --> 00:27:12,965
neither Jerry Mahoney,
Knucklehead Smith,

550
00:27:12,998 --> 00:27:14,133
or Farfel worked for years.

551
00:27:14,167 --> 00:27:17,070
Arthur Miller took it upon
himself to write

552
00:27:17,103 --> 00:27:20,107
an emotionally gut wrenching
commentary on the Cold War era

553
00:27:20,140 --> 00:27:21,742
called Topo Gigio
Goes to the Circus.

554
00:27:21,775 --> 00:27:23,075
Topo, by the way,

555
00:27:23,108 --> 00:27:25,078
used his earning
from the Ed Sullivan Show

556
00:27:25,112 --> 00:27:28,080
to fund the attempted
assassination of Fidel Castro.

557
00:27:28,113 --> 00:27:30,717
- Joel, after this,
nothing will ever show me again.

558
00:27:30,750 --> 00:27:32,018
- Oh, yeah? Brace yourself.

559
00:27:32,052 --> 00:27:33,420
'Cause the most sinister
friendly witness

560
00:27:33,453 --> 00:27:34,820
brought up in front
of the committee

561
00:27:34,853 --> 00:27:38,190
was none other than...Lamb Chop.

562
00:27:38,223 --> 00:27:39,892
- Oh, wow. Big surprise.

563
00:27:39,925 --> 00:27:41,895
- I always hated her anyway.

564
00:27:41,928 --> 00:27:45,165
- She was the one who put
the finger on Davey and Goliath.

565
00:27:45,198 --> 00:27:48,435
- Oh, not Davey and Goliath!
- Say it ain't so, Joel.

566
00:27:48,468 --> 00:27:49,702
Say it ain't so!

567
00:27:49,735 --> 00:27:50,770
- Davey,
being a God-fearing American

568
00:27:50,803 --> 00:27:52,305
was more than happy
to cooperate.

569
00:27:52,338 --> 00:27:54,107
Goliath named names too.

570
00:27:54,140 --> 00:27:59,378
- Uh, let's see,
there was Dolt and Trumble,

571
00:27:59,412 --> 00:28:02,315
Ringlaud Jr., Albert Maltz.

572
00:28:02,348 --> 00:28:04,317
and Berthold Brecht.

573
00:28:04,350 --> 00:28:07,287
- Unfortunately, all the
committee heard was arf! Arf!

574
00:28:07,320 --> 00:28:09,088
Arf! Arf! Arf!

575
00:28:09,122 --> 00:28:11,792
Goliath was convicted of
contempt of Congress

576
00:28:11,825 --> 00:28:13,092
and sent to the pound.

577
00:28:13,125 --> 00:28:15,195
- Well, at least we know
an error like that

578
00:28:15,228 --> 00:28:16,797
will never happen again.

579
00:28:16,830 --> 00:28:18,432
- That's right,
my little friends.

580
00:28:18,465 --> 00:28:20,900
- Yeah, I'm sure glad
I'm not a puppet.

581
00:28:20,933 --> 00:28:22,802
- Me too.
- Me neither.

582
00:28:22,835 --> 00:28:24,737
Who pulls your strings?

583
00:28:35,415 --> 00:28:36,783
Bubbises politicians?

584
00:28:36,817 --> 00:28:38,283
Sounds like
a Sid and Marty Krofft sitcom.

585
00:28:38,317 --> 00:28:43,823
- ...in the month of October
1957 was successfully placing

586
00:28:43,857 --> 00:28:45,525
an Earth satellite into orbit.

587
00:28:45,558 --> 00:28:47,493
In so doing, they changed

588
00:28:47,527 --> 00:28:50,863
the entire concept
of modern warfare.

589
00:28:50,897 --> 00:28:53,298
- And that hurts.
- Yeah.

590
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,503
- The story you are about
to see would be inevitable,

591
00:28:57,537 --> 00:29:01,240
should the wrong people gain
control of that government.

592
00:29:01,273 --> 00:29:04,243
- Like the new monkeys.
- Or the filmmakers.

593
00:29:04,277 --> 00:29:06,980
- Remember guys,
this is filmed in color.

594
00:29:07,013 --> 00:29:08,782
It's just that everything
is gray in Russia.

595
00:29:08,815 --> 00:29:10,450
- Ah.

596
00:29:10,483 --> 00:29:13,753
- Rocket Attack U.S.A.

597
00:29:16,588 --> 00:29:18,925
But it starts in Russia.

598
00:29:18,958 --> 00:29:23,897
Edward Czerniuk
as the Commie rat.

599
00:29:23,930 --> 00:29:26,398
Daniel Kern did the letters.

600
00:29:26,432 --> 00:29:28,367
- Hey, it's Art Metrano.

601
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:32,772
- [All singing]

602
00:29:34,607 --> 00:29:37,143
- Oh.

603
00:29:37,177 --> 00:29:39,212
- And the Godless Communists.

604
00:29:39,245 --> 00:29:42,315
- [Imitates Russian accent]
Vladovia Lazareff

605
00:29:42,348 --> 00:29:43,583
as the Commie scum.

606
00:29:43,617 --> 00:29:46,318
- Alan Smiler as a sad guy.

607
00:29:46,352 --> 00:29:48,988
Dietic photography by Mike Tab.

608
00:29:49,022 --> 00:29:53,827
- Short and one-named people
listed here.

609
00:29:56,462 --> 00:29:59,465
- Ceco, don't be a hero.

610
00:29:59,498 --> 00:30:01,968
- Rick Carrier pigeon.

611
00:30:02,002 --> 00:30:06,572
Big Steve Brody.
Baron Al Baron.

612
00:30:06,605 --> 00:30:10,243
- Ha ha. Next in Star Search

613
00:30:10,277 --> 00:30:13,947
opening credit competitions,
Sinbad.

614
00:30:13,980 --> 00:30:16,548
- And the people who made
this collection

615
00:30:16,582 --> 00:30:18,317
of stock footage possible.

616
00:30:18,350 --> 00:30:20,420
- Hey, check it out.
It's a water tower, you guys.

617
00:30:20,453 --> 00:30:21,653
- No, it's a flying tick.

618
00:30:21,687 --> 00:30:25,123
- It's a land-loving mother
pigeon of all phantom creeps.

619
00:30:25,157 --> 00:30:26,257
- Whoa.

620
00:30:26,290 --> 00:30:28,562
- No, I think it's a supersonic,
metal-covered spitball.

621
00:30:28,595 --> 00:30:29,928
- Meanwhile, I Love Lucy Shows

622
00:30:29,962 --> 00:30:32,065
are transmitted throughout
the world.

623
00:30:32,098 --> 00:30:35,100
- Hey look, that guy won a Tony.
- [Beacon-like noise]

624
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:36,067
- Cool.

625
00:30:36,100 --> 00:30:39,072
- Crickets.
I think I'll name my band that.

626
00:30:40,373 --> 00:30:43,175
- Hey, Morgan, come here
a minute and listen to this.

627
00:30:43,208 --> 00:30:44,943
- I can't. I'm busy right now.

628
00:30:44,977 --> 00:30:47,013
- Hey, he yelled all the way
from Washington.

629
00:30:47,047 --> 00:30:51,217
- That looks like Madison.
I'm on State Street.

630
00:30:51,250 --> 00:30:55,255
- ...eventually ended up
in the office

631
00:30:55,288 --> 00:30:57,223
of the Central Intelligence
group.

632
00:30:57,257 --> 00:30:58,490
- Heh, if you can believe that.

633
00:30:58,523 --> 00:31:02,062
- We believe it was fired from
a base in the Caspian Sea area.

634
00:31:02,095 --> 00:31:05,465
- We also believe that for every
drop of rain that falls,

635
00:31:05,498 --> 00:31:07,100
a flower grows.

636
00:31:07,133 --> 00:31:11,603
- ...we plotted each circuit
of the globe.

637
00:31:11,637 --> 00:31:13,505
- When this guy
quits passing gas,

638
00:31:13,538 --> 00:31:15,308
let's knock off for lunch. Shh.

639
00:31:15,342 --> 00:31:19,512
- ...96 minutes.
That's about all we know.

640
00:31:19,545 --> 00:31:23,617
Except that it is broadcasting
on two common frequencies.

641
00:31:23,650 --> 00:31:25,385
And the signal is not encoded.

642
00:31:25,418 --> 00:31:27,520
- And aliens are speaking
on my dental work.

643
00:31:27,553 --> 00:31:31,223
- ...is whether it is sending
out code while over Russia.

644
00:31:31,257 --> 00:31:35,193
And if it is, what information
it may be giving.

645
00:31:35,227 --> 00:31:36,260
- Line.

646
00:31:36,293 --> 00:31:38,932
- We think at the moment,
it may be nothing more

647
00:31:38,965 --> 00:31:40,300
than a device for propaganda.

648
00:31:40,333 --> 00:31:41,200
- How dare they!

649
00:31:41,233 --> 00:31:43,402
- But they may have
a big jump on us.

650
00:31:43,435 --> 00:31:47,407
- I'll let Dr. Hemlick explain.
- He makes more than me.

651
00:31:47,440 --> 00:31:48,540
- He's a good maneuverer

652
00:31:48,573 --> 00:31:49,675
- The intercontinental missile
program has been held up

653
00:31:49,708 --> 00:31:53,378
awaiting some way to get
a report on three vital points

654
00:31:53,412 --> 00:31:54,345
to do with outer space.

655
00:31:54,378 --> 00:31:56,215
- Geez, don't open with a joke
or anything.

656
00:31:56,248 --> 00:31:58,383
- One is the exact density
of the air at the altitude

657
00:31:58,417 --> 00:31:59,485
the missile will travel.

658
00:31:59,518 --> 00:32:01,220
- That's Doogie Howser
all grown up.

659
00:32:01,253 --> 00:32:03,522
- Second, the effect of
radioactivity on such a rocket.

660
00:32:03,555 --> 00:32:07,293
- I'm so ashamed.
- Third, do the other planets

661
00:32:07,327 --> 00:32:09,195
exert enough gravitational pull

662
00:32:09,228 --> 00:32:12,198
to cause deviation
in the navigation equipment?

663
00:32:12,232 --> 00:32:13,432
- Pause.

664
00:32:13,465 --> 00:32:16,102
- The whole purpose
of a world satellite

665
00:32:16,135 --> 00:32:17,970
is to find the answers
to these questions.

666
00:32:18,003 --> 00:32:19,272
- At the back of the book.

667
00:32:19,305 --> 00:32:23,475
- The first country
to obtain this information,

668
00:32:23,508 --> 00:32:25,378
will be able to fire
a successful missile

669
00:32:25,412 --> 00:32:27,447
- within six months.
- Well thanks, Bob,

670
00:32:27,480 --> 00:32:29,482
I think we all have
our cyanide tablets ready.

671
00:32:29,515 --> 00:32:30,315
- Pencil's down, people.

672
00:32:30,348 --> 00:32:31,550
- You may as well answer it,
General.

673
00:32:31,583 --> 00:32:32,685
That's all I have to say.

674
00:32:32,718 --> 00:32:36,122
- Oh, may I?
- Watkins here.

675
00:32:36,155 --> 00:32:38,457
- Yes, sir. Yes, sir.
- Three bags full.

676
00:32:38,490 --> 00:32:43,362
- Yes, sir. Pencil please.
- Give me a freakin' pencil.

677
00:32:43,395 --> 00:32:45,197
- Yes, sir.

678
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:49,168
- Go to Germany
and pick up my laundry.

679
00:32:49,202 --> 00:32:50,302
The political pressures

680
00:32:50,335 --> 00:32:52,205
regarding this matter
were terrific.

681
00:32:52,238 --> 00:32:54,073
- They were fabulous.
- Consequently, it wasn't long

682
00:32:54,107 --> 00:32:55,742
before a top secret
coded message

683
00:32:55,775 --> 00:32:57,610
reached the desk
of George White,

684
00:32:57,643 --> 00:32:59,312
chief of the European bureau...

685
00:32:59,345 --> 00:33:01,413
- It said,
"Drink more Ovaltine."

686
00:33:01,447 --> 00:33:05,752
- Get the laundry for you.
Blah, blah, blah.

687
00:33:05,785 --> 00:33:07,018
Hugs and kisses,
you know how it goes.

688
00:33:07,052 --> 00:33:09,088
Sign my name.

689
00:33:09,122 --> 00:33:11,490
- It's an ad for Dolphin temps.

690
00:33:11,523 --> 00:33:15,695
- Oh, let's check the daytimer.
Now, let's see.

691
00:33:15,728 --> 00:33:18,430
Massage at 2:30.
Manicure at 4:00.

692
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:19,630
Bum rush at 5:00.

693
00:33:19,663 --> 00:33:21,667
- Oh, yes, will you send him in.
I've been waiting for him.

694
00:33:21,700 --> 00:33:24,037
- Tell him I have his pencil.

695
00:33:27,140 --> 00:33:29,175
- How you doing, John?
- Hi, how are you, George?

696
00:33:29,208 --> 00:33:30,510
- Good to see you. Sit down.

697
00:33:30,543 --> 00:33:31,677
- You can have
the chair of shame.

698
00:33:31,710 --> 00:33:34,147
- Well, what's up?
- Your number, pink boy.

699
00:33:34,180 --> 00:33:36,515
- John, I sent for you because
you're the best agent we have.

700
00:33:36,548 --> 00:33:38,450
- What kind of collateral
can you give me?

701
00:33:38,483 --> 00:33:41,120
- What's the job? When you start
passing out compliments,

702
00:33:41,153 --> 00:33:43,122
I figure there's usually
something pretty big up.

703
00:33:43,155 --> 00:33:45,658
- Something big is up.
- Little man.

704
00:33:45,692 --> 00:33:49,495
- He looks like Sam Waterston.

705
00:33:49,528 --> 00:33:50,663
They want to find out
if the Reds are really

706
00:33:50,697 --> 00:33:52,632
on the right track
with these satellites.

707
00:33:52,665 --> 00:33:54,133
- Well, aren't these satellites

708
00:33:54,167 --> 00:33:56,502
just a good propaganda move
on their part?

709
00:33:56,535 --> 00:33:59,138
- We hope so. But they may be
getting data that would put them

710
00:33:59,172 --> 00:34:00,773
way ahead of us.

711
00:34:00,807 --> 00:34:03,175
Well, what do you want me to do?

712
00:34:03,208 --> 00:34:04,410
- You're gonna be flown
into Russia.

713
00:34:04,443 --> 00:34:05,612
Then make your way to Moscow.

714
00:34:05,645 --> 00:34:07,680
There you'll contact
one of our agents.

715
00:34:07,713 --> 00:34:11,217
- Then coat yourself with bear
grease and cluck like a chicken.

716
00:34:11,250 --> 00:34:13,552
- Incidentally, destroy
that after you've read it.

717
00:34:13,585 --> 00:34:16,355
- I'll throw it away.
- The agent is a girl.

718
00:34:16,388 --> 00:34:18,490
But we think that she's
the right one for the job.

719
00:34:18,523 --> 00:34:22,262
- Now I'll be the judge of that.
- That sounds interesting.

720
00:34:22,295 --> 00:34:23,662
- Remember, John,
we want information,

721
00:34:23,695 --> 00:34:26,398
not a repetition of that affair
in Vienna.

722
00:34:26,432 --> 00:34:29,268
- My lederhosen just came back
from the cleaners.

723
00:34:29,302 --> 00:34:31,303
- Seriously, George,
when do I go?

724
00:34:31,337 --> 00:34:33,172
- Right now. I'll drive you to

725
00:34:33,205 --> 00:34:34,407
a little airport
outside of town.

726
00:34:34,440 --> 00:34:35,875
One of our best pilots
will take you

727
00:34:35,908 --> 00:34:37,543
in a special civilian plane.

728
00:34:37,577 --> 00:34:39,312
- And then push you
out the back screaming.

729
00:34:39,345 --> 00:34:41,313
I have no need to tell you,
if you're caught,

730
00:34:41,347 --> 00:34:42,715
we'll have to say that we never
