- [Applause]

973
00:46:30,355 --> 00:46:34,025
- All right, let's get things
started with a toss-up question.

974
00:46:34,058 --> 00:46:38,263
What three-word slogan was
coined during the Cold War

975
00:46:38,297 --> 00:46:43,102
as a schoolchild's best defense
against a A-bomb attack?

976
00:46:43,135 --> 00:46:44,603
- [Buzzer]
- Tom Servo of Oak Ridge.

977
00:46:44,637 --> 00:46:46,572
- Duck and cover?
- Could you state that

978
00:46:46,605 --> 00:46:48,107
in the form of a desperate
cry to God

979
00:46:48,140 --> 00:46:50,108
to save you
from an unholy death, please?

980
00:46:50,142 --> 00:46:53,045
- Duck and cover!
- He's right for five points.

981
00:46:53,078 --> 00:46:54,580
- Thank you. I'll take
propaganda for 15, please.

982
00:46:54,613 --> 00:46:58,083
- Category is propaganda.
Complete the following sentence.

983
00:46:58,117 --> 00:47:01,320
Had this been
an actual emergency...

984
00:47:01,353 --> 00:47:03,955
- [Buzzer]
- Oh! Crow from Los Alamos.

985
00:47:03,988 --> 00:47:06,325
- Uh, you would have been
advised to tune in to

986
00:47:06,358 --> 00:47:07,927
the emergency broadcast station
in your area

987
00:47:07,960 --> 00:47:09,393
for further information.

988
00:47:09,427 --> 00:47:12,430
- Is right. And there's
a bonus burst of strontium 90

989
00:47:12,463 --> 00:47:15,133
if you can complete
the rest of the message.

990
00:47:15,167 --> 00:47:18,603
- Uh, don't tell me.
I know this.

991
00:47:18,637 --> 00:47:20,872
And this concludes the test

992
00:47:20,905 --> 00:47:22,240
of the emergency broadcast
system?

993
00:47:22,273 --> 00:47:25,477
- Right. So Crow from Los Alamos
is off to an early lead

994
00:47:25,510 --> 00:47:29,180
with 30 rems of radiation.
Time for another toss up.

995
00:47:29,213 --> 00:47:32,283
Which early '60s
CIA covert operation

996
00:47:32,317 --> 00:47:35,587
was considered their greatest
failure of the decade?

997
00:47:35,620 --> 00:47:37,422
- [Buzzer]
- Crow, Los Alamos.

998
00:47:37,455 --> 00:47:38,688
- The Kennedy assassination.

999
00:47:38,722 --> 00:47:41,960
- No, I'm afraid that one was
a success. Tom Servo, Oak Ridge.

1000
00:47:41,993 --> 00:47:44,630
- The Bay of Pigs.
- Is right for 20 rems.

1001
00:47:44,663 --> 00:47:46,332
- Thank you.

1002
00:47:46,365 --> 00:47:47,465
I'll take civil defense myths
and legends for 50, please.

1003
00:47:47,498 --> 00:47:53,205
- Ooh. Taking a gamble
for 50 curies of polonium. Wow.

1004
00:47:53,238 --> 00:47:56,342
Here we go with civil defense
myths and legends.

1005
00:47:56,375 --> 00:47:59,310
- How do you protect yourself
from fallout?

1006
00:47:59,343 --> 00:48:02,313
A) Hide in the basement
until it goes away.

1007
00:48:02,347 --> 00:48:04,582
B) Wear protective
rubber underwear

1008
00:48:04,615 --> 00:48:07,418
and simply brush yourself off
at the end of the day.

1009
00:48:07,452 --> 00:48:10,955
Or C) Run naked through
a field of sorghum.

1010
00:48:10,988 --> 00:48:12,923
- A and B.
- Is right.

1011
00:48:12,957 --> 00:48:15,360
- Thank you. I'd like to stake
five curies and bottle.

1012
00:48:15,393 --> 00:48:19,330
- Oh, we're living dangerously.
Tom Servo of Oak Ridge.

1013
00:48:19,363 --> 00:48:23,535
- Name the two most famous myths
of civil defense.

1014
00:48:23,568 --> 00:48:26,170
- We can win a nuclear war.
- Number one.

1015
00:48:26,203 --> 00:48:28,273
- And survivors
are the lucky ones.

1016
00:48:28,307 --> 00:48:31,543
- Right. And Tom Servo from
Oak Ridge rockets into the lead

1017
00:48:31,577 --> 00:48:33,978
with 15 curies and 20 rems.

1018
00:48:34,012 --> 00:48:35,347
[Air raid alarms sounding]

1019
00:48:35,380 --> 00:48:38,017
- Uh oh, It's time for
the fallout elimination round.

1020
00:48:38,050 --> 00:48:39,283
The winner will receive

1021
00:48:39,317 --> 00:48:42,053
a complete thermolactic nuclear
fallout shelter,

1022
00:48:42,087 --> 00:48:44,455
complete with a 17-year supply
of canned food

1023
00:48:44,488 --> 00:48:48,393
and 500,000 cloritin
water purification tablets.

1024
00:48:48,427 --> 00:48:51,463
There are two questions.
Number one--

1025
00:48:51,497 --> 00:48:53,565
The category
is Hollywood Legends.

1026
00:48:53,598 --> 00:48:58,137
Name the movie with the most
radioactive and deadly set.

1027
00:48:58,170 --> 00:48:59,070
- [Buzzer]
- Gypsy.

1028
00:48:59,103 --> 00:49:01,205
- Moby Dick
with Richard Baseheart.

1029
00:49:01,238 --> 00:49:04,442
- Oh, I'm sorry.
You're out of the game.

1030
00:49:04,475 --> 00:49:06,378
Crow from Los Alamos.

1031
00:49:06,412 --> 00:49:08,180
- Uh, The Conqueror.
- Is correct.

1032
00:49:08,213 --> 00:49:11,015
Just ask John Wayne
or Agnes Morehead.

1033
00:49:11,048 --> 00:49:13,452
Now on to our final question.

1034
00:49:13,485 --> 00:49:15,453
What is the most practical thing
you can do

1035
00:49:15,487 --> 00:49:18,157
in the event of a total
thermonuclear war?

1036
00:49:18,190 --> 00:49:19,657
- [Buzzer]
- Tom Servo of Oak Ridge.

1037
00:49:19,690 --> 00:49:21,427
- Duck and cover!
- Wrong.

1038
00:49:21,460 --> 00:49:24,195
- Crow, Los Alamos.
- Stick your head--

1039
00:49:24,228 --> 00:49:26,998
- We got movie. Aah!

1040
00:49:37,375 --> 00:49:39,110
- Hey, it's been 13 years.

1041
00:49:39,143 --> 00:49:40,412
You can come out of
that closet now.

1042
00:49:40,445 --> 00:49:42,480
- Joel, who won
the quiz bowl anyway?

1043
00:49:42,513 --> 00:49:47,385
- I think we all won.
We live in a democracy.

1044
00:49:47,418 --> 00:49:49,153
- [Retches]

1045
00:49:54,760 --> 00:49:56,293
- We missed the best part.

1046
00:49:56,327 --> 00:49:58,630
- That was really horrible
as usual.

1047
00:49:58,663 --> 00:49:59,497
The second time was worse.

1048
00:49:59,530 --> 00:50:01,232
And the third time
was really tough.

1049
00:50:01,265 --> 00:50:02,800
- Don't worry about me.
I'm okay.

1050
00:50:02,833 --> 00:50:05,637
Looks like you got
the worst of it.

1051
00:50:05,670 --> 00:50:07,538
- Thanks, I think.

1052
00:50:09,540 --> 00:50:12,243
- All right, tell me.
Did you find out anything?

1053
00:50:12,277 --> 00:50:14,045
- Well, he's got bad skin.

1054
00:50:14,078 --> 00:50:15,580
He's got a mole shaped
like Nixon.

1055
00:50:15,613 --> 00:50:18,450
- There has been a split
in the presidium.

1056
00:50:18,483 --> 00:50:22,387
Tomorrow, the military are going
to push for a showdown.

1057
00:50:22,420 --> 00:50:25,390
The want to strike as soon as
the missile is finished

1058
00:50:25,423 --> 00:50:28,260
And while your country is still
unable to retaliate.

1059
00:50:28,293 --> 00:50:29,660
- Funny. All I heard was.
Uh, you got my leg.

1060
00:50:29,693 --> 00:50:34,532
- I don't know. A lot depends
on the vote tomorrow.

1061
00:50:34,565 --> 00:50:36,367
- Voting in Russia?

1062
00:50:36,400 --> 00:50:38,737
- Hard to believe
that a group of civilized men

1063
00:50:38,770 --> 00:50:40,438
could sit around
and calmly discuss

1064
00:50:40,472 --> 00:50:42,540
how to murder five
or six million others.

1065
00:50:42,573 --> 00:50:45,277
- That's why
we've got to crush them!

1066
00:50:45,310 --> 00:50:47,478
- ...ahead of the world.
Such a power can do

1067
00:50:47,512 --> 00:50:49,380
unbelievable things.

1068
00:50:49,413 --> 00:50:51,783
- Yeah, I heard. Go ahead.
Rub it in.

1069
00:50:51,817 --> 00:50:53,152
- You maybe picked
the wrong side.

1070
00:50:53,185 --> 00:50:54,685
- Don't touch me there.

1071
00:50:54,718 --> 00:50:56,822
- I'm happy it was my choice.

1072
00:50:56,855 --> 00:50:59,123
- Huh?

1073
00:50:59,157 --> 00:51:00,458
[Cheering]

1074
00:51:00,492 --> 00:51:03,862
- ♪ Look for the Union Label ♪

1075
00:51:03,895 --> 00:51:06,297
[Humming]

1076
00:51:06,330 --> 00:51:09,133
- [Speaking Russian]
- Spit it out, man.

1077
00:51:09,167 --> 00:51:13,705
- ...to consider the use of
Sputnik for military purposes.

1078
00:51:13,738 --> 00:51:17,608
He now turns to the minister of
economy and asks his opinion.

1079
00:51:17,642 --> 00:51:22,847
The minister replies
that the economy can stand

1080
00:51:22,880 --> 00:51:24,448
a war at this time.

1081
00:51:24,482 --> 00:51:25,850
- Suddenly,
Ivan tells a salty joke

1082
00:51:25,883 --> 00:51:27,652
involving an elephant
and a ladder.

1083
00:51:27,685 --> 00:51:29,753
- I can't repeat it.
But the punch line is what?

1084
00:51:29,787 --> 00:51:30,853
To give up show business?

1085
00:51:30,887 --> 00:51:33,858
- Marshall now asks his staff
and defense minister

1086
00:51:33,892 --> 00:51:35,727
if they concur with him in this.

1087
00:51:35,760 --> 00:51:38,730
- George Johnson Jr. says da.

1088
00:51:38,763 --> 00:51:40,698
- However, the other ministers
do not agree.

1089
00:51:40,732 --> 00:51:42,733
The marshall is very annoyed.

1090
00:51:42,767 --> 00:51:45,470
- And he loses a pen.

1091
00:51:45,503 --> 00:51:46,337
- ...why they feel they can't go
along with him in this action.

1092
00:51:46,370 --> 00:51:49,440
He emphasizes
that he has the punch,

1093
00:51:49,473 --> 00:51:52,310
the advantage of surprise,
and the first rockets.

1094
00:51:52,343 --> 00:51:56,213
- Boris picks up a sand wedge
and moves to the map.

1095
00:51:56,247 --> 00:51:59,517
- ...would give full
and absolute control of America

1096
00:51:59,550 --> 00:52:00,517
to them.

1097
00:52:00,550 --> 00:52:04,155
- Looks like Boy George.
- Yeah. Same costume.

1098
00:52:04,188 --> 00:52:07,625
- Our agent from Moscow has
gotten this information through.

1099
00:52:07,658 --> 00:52:09,862
- But I didn't think Moscow
believed in tears.

1100
00:52:09,895 --> 00:52:11,630
- ...Kremlin, the militarists.

1101
00:52:11,663 --> 00:52:14,565
And they're considering
launching a missile

1102
00:52:14,598 --> 00:52:15,867
as soon as they can

1103
00:52:15,900 --> 00:52:17,335
- Now when can you start work?

1104
00:52:17,368 --> 00:52:18,770
- I'll lay my cards
on the table.

1105
00:52:18,803 --> 00:52:20,238
- Go fish.

1106
00:52:20,272 --> 00:52:21,472
- If we can't come up
with something better,

1107
00:52:21,505 --> 00:52:24,708
within in a reasonable time,
this country is going to witness

1108
00:52:24,742 --> 00:52:27,312
the most frightful disaster
it has ever seen.

1109
00:52:27,345 --> 00:52:29,413
- You mean an actor
becoming president?

1110
00:52:29,447 --> 00:52:31,917
- William Shatner will direct.

1111
00:52:31,950 --> 00:52:33,685
- Hmm. No.

1112
00:52:33,718 --> 00:52:36,387
- With the problem of the
skin friction on the warhead,

1113
00:52:36,420 --> 00:52:39,723
- Are we talking about
the same thing here, buddy?

1114
00:52:39,757 --> 00:52:42,527
- We can modify
the navigational equipment.

1115
00:52:42,560 --> 00:52:43,727
- And we're working on a device

1116
00:52:43,760 --> 00:52:46,598
to protect the rocket from
meteorites and radioactivity.

1117
00:52:46,632 --> 00:52:49,400
Give us time.
We will give you your missile.

1118
00:52:49,433 --> 00:52:52,770
- Time? We have no time.
- For sergeants. Heh.

1119
00:52:52,803 --> 00:52:55,240
- Had we half the money
that was appropriated

1120
00:52:55,273 --> 00:52:58,277
for such worthy projects
as cheese price support,

1121
00:52:58,310 --> 00:53:00,645
we would've had our ICBM
long ago.

1122
00:53:00,678 --> 00:53:03,715
- Hmm.
Someone's a little bitter.

1123
00:53:03,748 --> 00:53:05,283
- ...peacetime general
is up against.

1124
00:53:05,317 --> 00:53:08,487
But actually at any rate,

1125
00:53:08,520 --> 00:53:11,555
you now have all the resources
of the country behind you.

1126
00:53:11,588 --> 00:53:12,990
- And me.

1127
00:53:13,023 --> 00:53:16,895
- And God grant
that we may not be too late.

1128
00:53:16,928 --> 00:53:20,732
- Whoa.
Beach bluff makes Tar woozy.

1129
00:53:20,765 --> 00:53:22,967
- [Muttering in Russian]

1130
00:53:23,000 --> 00:53:26,403
- The fat, drunken Russian pig
asks for a Long Island Ice Tea.

1131
00:53:26,437 --> 00:53:30,642
- Oh, my lack of God.
I feel sick.

1132
00:53:30,675 --> 00:53:33,243
- Come,
let me tell you something.

1133
00:53:33,277 --> 00:53:34,745
[Retches]

1134
00:53:34,778 --> 00:53:38,283
- Dear Penthouse, I never
thought I'd be writing you.

1135
00:53:38,317 --> 00:53:42,387
But I met this college girl
from Minsk.

1136
00:53:42,420 --> 00:53:44,455
- Watch it.
- Hey, that's not scotch
guarded.

1137
00:53:44,488 --> 00:53:46,457
Please!

1138
00:53:46,490 --> 00:53:49,627
- All right, all done in there?

1139
00:53:51,797 --> 00:53:55,333
- He's asleep. When he's like
this, nothing can awake him.

1140
00:53:55,367 --> 00:53:58,437
- Really? Well, let me try.

1141
00:53:58,470 --> 00:54:00,372
- We're going to have
to get him his own room.

1142
00:54:00,405 --> 00:54:01,873
He's just getting too big.

1143
00:54:01,907 --> 00:54:05,743
- I wish I knew what was going
on inside that brain of his.

1144
00:54:05,777 --> 00:54:07,678
- Well, let's crack it open
and take a look.

1145
00:54:07,712 --> 00:54:10,848
- He's going on an inspection
trip to the missile site.

1146
00:54:10,882 --> 00:54:12,817
And he wants me to go with him.

1147
00:54:12,850 --> 00:54:14,518
- Could you say that
a little more woodenly?

1148
00:54:14,552 --> 00:54:15,687
- He leaves on the train
in the morning.

1149
00:54:15,720 --> 00:54:17,688
That's why the celebration
tonight.

1150
00:54:17,722 --> 00:54:19,890
- Yeah,
looks like he's having a ball.

1151
00:54:19,923 --> 00:54:23,362
- The saddest excuse, he drinks.
When he's drunk, he wants me.

1152
00:54:23,395 --> 00:54:28,433
Not for a nice party or dinner.
But only here.

1153
00:54:28,467 --> 00:54:30,368
- What's wrong with that?

1154
00:54:30,402 --> 00:54:32,003
- Why can't he go home
to his wife?

1155
00:54:32,037 --> 00:54:34,738
- Because she's
fat and bald too.

1156
00:54:34,772 --> 00:54:37,675
Sometimes when I'm in that
closet and he's out here,

1157
00:54:37,708 --> 00:54:39,643
I'd like to come out here and--

1158
00:54:39,677 --> 00:54:42,513
- Put you in the closet.
- It has to be.

1159
00:54:42,547 --> 00:54:44,815
I really don't mind.

1160
00:54:44,848 --> 00:54:46,985
- But I do.

1161
00:54:47,018 --> 00:54:49,453
- What? What?

1162
00:54:49,487 --> 00:54:51,790
- Something--oh. Okay.

1163
00:54:51,823 --> 00:54:54,825
Oh, you taste like a fat,
drunk Russian.

1164
00:54:57,762 --> 00:54:58,730
Neat.

1165
00:55:00,030 --> 00:55:01,800
- What's that supposed to mean?

1166
00:55:01,833 --> 00:55:05,037
- Far to the north lay this
fully operational missile base.

1167
00:55:05,070 --> 00:55:06,905
- Or telephone pole.

1168
00:55:06,938 --> 00:55:10,108
- ...an intercontinental missile
capable of completely destroying

1169
00:55:10,142 --> 00:55:12,943
a city thousands of miles
distant.

1170
00:55:12,977 --> 00:55:14,812
- Yeah, sure.
- Neat, huh?

1171
00:55:14,845 --> 00:55:17,782
- [Speaking Russian]

1172
00:55:17,815 --> 00:55:20,085
- You doorman or waiter?

1173
00:55:20,118 --> 00:55:23,522
Monkey boy. Me Monkey Boy.

1174
00:55:23,555 --> 00:55:26,657
Doh, the missile good.
Bring me potato sandwich

1175
00:55:26,690 --> 00:55:30,695
with mush on top.
Out of here.

1176
00:55:30,728 --> 00:55:32,430
- [Humming]

1177
00:55:43,007 --> 00:55:44,442
- Oops.
- Aim, fire.

1178
00:55:44,475 --> 00:55:46,343
[Imitating gunfire]

1179
00:55:48,078 --> 00:55:50,648
- Would you like to kiss
the missile?

1180
00:55:50,682 --> 00:55:53,985
- The captain compliments
the colonel

1181
00:55:54,018 --> 00:55:57,855
on his well pressed suit
and asks him where he got it.

1182
00:55:57,888 --> 00:55:59,423
- Just when we need
the voiceover,

1183
00:55:59,457 --> 00:56:00,958
it's nowhere to be seen.

1184
00:56:00,992 --> 00:56:04,862
- Of course, I'm going
to steam off the wallpaper

1185
00:56:04,895 --> 00:56:06,798
and repaint the whole thing
all around.

1186
00:56:06,832 --> 00:56:09,600
Some nice molding to warm it up
a little, but that's it.

1187
00:56:09,633 --> 00:56:12,003
- It must be a stealth missile.
There's no shadow.

1188
00:56:12,037 --> 00:56:13,672
- How's the missile?

1189
00:56:13,705 --> 00:56:18,408
- Well, I've seen plenty.
- Seen enough.

1190
00:56:18,442 --> 00:56:19,877
- Placebo.
- Yep.

1191
00:56:19,910 --> 00:56:22,880
- Eh. Let's got get some chow.
- Hey, good idea.

1192
00:56:22,913 --> 00:56:25,983
- I'm just going
to get a whole pie.

1193
00:56:26,017 --> 00:56:29,453
[Crosstalk]

1194
00:56:32,490 --> 00:56:36,593
- Sundown in the union. Whoo.

1195
00:56:36,627 --> 00:56:39,097
- Hope I can sell this
"Doctor, Doctor" spec script.

1196
00:56:39,130 --> 00:56:41,432
[Phone rings]

1197
00:56:41,465 --> 00:56:43,468
- Watkins.

1198
00:56:43,502 --> 00:56:46,603
- Oh, hi, dear.
- It's the president.

1199
00:56:46,637 --> 00:56:49,640
- No, why don't you go ahead
and eat without me.

1200
00:56:49,673 --> 00:56:52,177
We're testing tomorrow.

1201
00:56:52,210 --> 00:56:54,212
- The MMPI.
- I'll be here all night

1202
00:56:54,245 --> 00:56:56,080
finishing this paperwork.

1203
00:56:56,113 --> 00:56:58,650
- Oh, really? No, can't do that.

1204
00:56:58,683 --> 00:57:00,050
- Well, why don't you and Helen
take in a movie?

1205
00:57:00,083 --> 00:57:02,453
- 'Cause Helen's my imaginary
friend. She's not real.

1206
00:57:02,487 --> 00:57:03,955
You keep doing this to me.

1207
00:57:03,988 --> 00:57:07,125
- Honey, I just have to get
these reports out tonight.

1208
00:57:07,158 --> 00:57:09,660
- What kind of reports? What are
you, seeing other women?

1209
00:57:09,693 --> 00:57:12,130
- We've been through
all this before.

1210
00:57:12,163 --> 00:57:16,067
- Well, I got to get going.
- Okay, goodnight.

1211
00:57:16,100 --> 00:57:17,735
- Idiot.

1212
00:57:17,768 --> 00:57:20,037
- So we were just switching
to aid our controls.

1213
00:57:20,070 --> 00:57:21,638
And everything went boom.

1214
00:57:21,672 --> 00:57:23,140
- Could you put that
into laymen's terms, please?

1215
00:57:23,173 --> 00:57:25,042
- That does not mean we do not
have a successful missile

1216
00:57:25,075 --> 00:57:27,512
that can pinpoint
a target 6,000 miles away.

1217
00:57:27,545 --> 00:57:28,947
It was just bad luck.

1218
00:57:28,980 --> 00:57:31,950
- Bad luck?
That's putting it mildly.

1219
00:57:31,983 --> 00:57:34,685
- I was counting very heavily
on this.

1220
00:57:34,718 --> 00:57:37,088
- I nearly expressed
an emotion back there.

1221
00:57:37,122 --> 00:57:39,757
- One more test will prove it.

1222
00:57:39,790 --> 00:57:41,893
- In two weeks,
we'll be ready to try again.

1223
00:57:41,927 --> 00:57:44,795
- Two weeks?
We don't have two weeks.

1224
00:57:44,828 --> 00:57:46,597
- I'm going on vacation
next week.

1225
00:57:46,630 --> 00:57:48,833
- If this test
had been successful,

1226
00:57:48,867 --> 00:57:51,835
I really think we had a chance
to go ahead of the Reds.

1227
00:57:51,868 --> 00:57:53,905
But now the fate of this country

1228
00:57:53,938 --> 00:57:56,140
is hanging on a very thin
thread.

1229
00:57:56,173 --> 00:57:58,710
- And suddenly I'm the jerk.

1230
00:57:58,743 --> 00:58:03,882
- ...our chance at sabotage.

1231
00:58:04,815 --> 00:58:08,185
[Inhales, knocking at door]

1232
00:58:08,218 --> 00:58:09,953
- Come out.

1233
00:58:09,987 --> 00:58:12,623
- All done? Honey?

1234
00:58:18,228 --> 00:58:19,830
- Hmm.

1235
00:58:24,635 --> 00:58:27,205
- Hey, Kiba!
I asked for extra cheese.

1236
00:58:27,238 --> 00:58:29,740
- I want $3.00 off.
- Who are you? What do you want?

1237
00:58:29,773 --> 00:58:32,010
- Bill Bixby,
what are you doing here?

1238
00:58:32,043 --> 00:58:34,845
- Look at the note.
- Like that'll prove anything.

1239
00:58:34,878 --> 00:58:38,817
- Nice shoes. Hush puppies huh?
I like you.

1240
00:58:38,850 --> 00:58:41,885
- What's that supposed to mean?

1241
00:58:41,918 --> 00:58:44,988
- Hmm.

1242
00:58:45,022 --> 00:58:47,025
- Let me see here. There was
an old hermit named Dave

1243
00:58:47,058 --> 00:58:49,893
who lived by himself in cave.
This doesn't prove anything.

1244
00:58:49,927 --> 00:58:52,230
What's this supposed to mean?

1245
00:58:52,263 --> 00:58:54,632
- Where is she?
- It's me honey.

1246
00:58:54,665 --> 00:58:56,833
Nice disguise, huh?

1247
00:58:56,867 --> 00:58:57,833
- How?
- By train.

1248
00:58:57,867 --> 00:59:02,040
We can make the journey
in several short trips.

1249
00:59:02,073 --> 00:59:05,977
- That way there'll be
no fatigue.

1250
00:59:06,010 --> 00:59:07,745
- There's a train that leaves
at 8:30.

1251
00:59:07,778 --> 00:59:10,213
- Be under it.
- I've got the tickets.

1252
00:59:10,247 --> 00:59:11,748
Will you come?

1253
00:59:11,782 --> 00:59:14,185
- Well, I like your key.
Yes, I'll come.

1254
00:59:14,218 --> 00:59:16,187
- I guess I have to trust you.
- Sure, why not.

1255
00:59:16,220 --> 00:59:18,122
- Name's Johnny Manston.

1256
00:59:18,155 --> 00:59:20,592
- Lawrence Steele,
British intelligence.

1257
00:59:20,625 --> 00:59:22,293
- Isn't that an oxymoron?

1258
00:59:22,327 --> 00:59:23,795
- How did you get mixed up
in this?

1259
00:59:23,828 --> 00:59:26,063
- We like to keep our hand
in everything.

1260
00:59:26,097 --> 00:59:29,067
- Ahem.
- Actually, we've been trying

1261
00:59:29,100 --> 00:59:30,935
to make contact with Tanya
ever since she took up with--

1262
00:59:30,968 --> 00:59:33,303
- Tanya? Surely you mean
Patti Hurst.

1263
00:59:33,337 --> 00:59:34,805
- I had my chance on the train.

1264
00:59:34,838 --> 00:59:37,908
She says that it's imperative
that you join her.

1265
00:59:37,942 --> 00:59:39,943
- Join her.
- That's what the note's for.

1266
00:59:39,977 --> 00:59:43,180
- Don't tell me what the note's
about, you squinty-eyed--

1267
00:59:43,213 --> 00:59:47,218
- Well, I'm glad you made it.
She says they're about ready

1268
00:59:47,252 --> 00:59:49,620
to fire the missile.

1269
00:59:49,653 --> 00:59:51,322
- We'd better go then.

1270
00:59:51,355 --> 00:59:52,923
- We'd better.
- Okay.

1271
00:59:52,957 --> 00:59:53,923
I guess I haven't got time

1272
00:59:53,957 --> 00:59:56,193
to wait for any further orders
from Germany.

1273
00:59:56,227 --> 00:59:59,130
- Limey, this could be the start
of a beautiful friendship.

1274
00:59:59,163 --> 01:00:03,100
- This is the U.S. cushion,
inferior to ours in many ways.

1275
01:00:03,133 --> 01:00:04,768
- Newspapers are informing
the world

1276
01:00:04,802 --> 01:00:07,338
that the American attempts
to launch their rocket--

1277
01:00:07,372 --> 01:00:09,807
- Ivan attempts
to sell Amway products

1278
01:00:09,840 --> 01:00:12,843
to his unwitting guests.

1279
01:00:12,877 --> 01:00:15,380
- ...and finalize their new
military position

1280
01:00:15,413 --> 01:00:17,315
of the Russian rockets.

1281
01:00:17,348 --> 01:00:21,018
- And now he's bragging about
his night with the girl.

1282
01:00:26,957 --> 01:00:30,093
- We turn you over now
to Mike Wallace in the field.

1283
01:00:30,127 --> 01:00:31,895
- I'm standing
in a shotgun shack

1284
01:00:31,928 --> 01:00:33,798
in another part of the world.

1285
01:00:33,832 --> 01:00:37,635
But this is not
my beautiful wife. Huh?

1286
01:00:38,903 --> 01:00:40,405
- This used to be a comedy club.

1287
01:00:40,438 --> 01:00:43,875
- But then Kipa Dad let loose
and the audience went way down.

1288
01:00:43,908 --> 01:00:46,010
- All done in there?
- Careful.

1289
01:00:50,313 --> 01:00:52,750
- He's over there.

1290
01:00:52,783 --> 01:00:54,285
This is as far as I go.

1291
01:00:54,318 --> 01:00:55,853
- I wanted to go farther.

1292
01:00:55,887 --> 01:00:57,087
But I'm not a fat, drunk,
bald Russian pig.

1293
01:00:57,120 --> 01:01:00,925
- He's right. You see these
woods are so filled with guards,

1294
01:01:00,958 --> 01:01:02,293
alone he'll stand
a better chance.

1295
01:01:02,327 --> 01:01:04,395
- I'd love to hang around
for all the danger,

1296
01:01:04,428 --> 01:01:07,132
but I got an early 8:00.
I got to go.

1297
01:01:07,165 --> 01:01:08,332
- Give him this.
- It's raw pork.

1298
01:01:08,365 --> 01:01:10,267
- He'll know what it means.
- Thank you.

1299
01:01:10,300 --> 01:01:12,203
Will you be contacting us again?

1300
01:01:12,237 --> 01:01:15,172
- From the grave?
I don't think so.

1301
01:01:15,205 --> 01:01:16,807
Tell him to be careful.

1302
01:01:16,840 --> 01:01:18,342
You know,
I sort of like the guy.

1303
01:01:18,375 --> 01:01:20,412
- A lot.

1304
01:01:20,445 --> 01:01:23,782
- Tell Lara I love her. Bye.

1305
01:01:23,815 --> 01:01:26,283
- Oh, God. I hate him.

1306
01:01:28,052 --> 01:01:30,888
- Hey, you have fun
with your friend?

1307
01:01:30,922 --> 01:01:33,157
- Hi.
- Johnny.

1308
01:01:33,190 --> 01:01:34,992
- Hello.

1309
01:01:35,025 --> 01:01:37,128
- You're safe. I was worried.

1310
01:01:37,162 --> 01:01:39,063
The police are thick
in this area.

1311
01:01:39,097 --> 01:01:41,198
- Oh, we had
a few rough moments.

1312
01:01:41,232 --> 01:01:44,002
- But we couldn't afford
to show them in the film.

1313
01:01:44,035 --> 01:01:45,235
- About 50 bucks
for all the raw pork.

1314
01:01:45,268 --> 01:01:48,172
- Why did you send him back?
- Because it's a one-man job.

1315
01:01:48,205 --> 01:01:50,340
- It'd be tough enough for one
let alone two.

1316
01:01:50,373 --> 01:01:54,178
- Don't let this building
be a metaphor for our love.

1317
01:01:54,212 --> 01:01:55,312
- ...New York tonight.

1318
01:01:55,345 --> 01:01:57,248
- New York? Hmm. That doesn't
fit into my schedule.

1319
01:01:57,282 --> 01:01:58,883
- Aren't they afraid of
retaliation?

1320
01:01:58,917 --> 01:02:00,818
- The test missile crashed
on take off.

1321
01:02:00,852 --> 01:02:03,453
- Ladies and gentlemen,
enjoy the only arty shot

1322
01:02:03,487 --> 01:02:05,288
in the entire film.

1323
01:02:05,322 --> 01:02:06,122
- We don't need rockets.

1324
01:02:06,155 --> 01:02:08,058
Our Air Force can still
drop bombs.

1325
01:02:08,092 --> 01:02:09,293
- The Kremlin is positive

1326
01:02:09,327 --> 01:02:11,162
that none of your aircraft
can get through.

1327
01:02:11,195 --> 01:02:15,767
Our anti-aircraft rockets
have proven 100% effective.

1328
01:02:15,800 --> 01:02:17,235
- Did Lawrence give you the TNT?

1329
01:02:17,268 --> 01:02:19,870
- He gave me the T and the N,
but not the other T.

1330
01:02:19,903 --> 01:02:21,838
And I had the A.

1331
01:02:23,540 --> 01:02:27,478
- That's okay. You didn't have
to get me anything.

1332
01:02:27,512 --> 01:02:30,280
Sandwiches?

1333
01:02:30,313 --> 01:02:31,848
- Now pork.

1334
01:02:33,918 --> 01:02:37,255
- Where's the site from here?
- I'll show you.

1335
01:02:37,288 --> 01:02:39,423
- I told you
I must do it by myself.

1336
01:02:39,457 --> 01:02:42,793
- Okay, find it yourself,
you big spy dink.

1337
01:02:44,362 --> 01:02:47,565
- I've got to go with you
no matter what happens.

1338
01:02:47,598 --> 01:02:49,400
Do you understand?

1339
01:02:49,433 --> 01:02:51,135
- But you'll get
the worst of it.

1340
01:02:51,168 --> 01:02:52,570
- I think I do.

1341
01:02:52,603 --> 01:02:54,872
- Look, you're getting
on that plane.

1342
01:02:54,905 --> 01:02:56,273
And we'll always have Paris.

1343
01:02:56,307 --> 01:02:58,542
But, uh,
I've always wanted to say that.

1344
01:02:58,575 --> 01:03:01,145
- Thank you, Woody.
- All right.

1345
01:03:01,178 --> 01:03:03,247
- Now after you.
- No, after you.

1346
01:03:03,280 --> 01:03:04,482
- No, you go first.
- I insist.

1347
01:03:04,515 --> 01:03:06,350
- Age before beauty.
- Ladies first.

1348
01:03:09,153 --> 01:03:11,855
So this music sure implies

1349
01:03:11,888 --> 01:03:13,457
something exciting going
to happen.

1350
01:03:13,490 --> 01:03:15,058
- The end?

1351
01:03:15,092 --> 01:03:17,928
- Ah, couldn't be that one.

1352
01:03:17,962 --> 01:03:19,863
Very exciting.

1353
01:03:21,265 --> 01:03:23,400
You know,
I kind of like that Sting guy.

1354
01:03:23,433 --> 01:03:26,170
He did that song about us
and our children.

1355
01:03:26,203 --> 01:03:27,905
He didn't have to do that.

1356
01:03:27,938 --> 01:03:29,340
- They can't wait.

1357
01:03:32,277 --> 01:03:34,945
Say, do I look like
Ronnie McDowell?

1358
01:03:36,947 --> 01:03:39,917
- Oy.
- What do you mean by oy?

1359
01:03:39,950 --> 01:03:42,185
- [Speaking Russian]

1360
01:03:42,218 --> 01:03:43,453
- Really, old boy.
I don't know what you mean.

1361
01:03:43,487 --> 01:03:45,422
Nobody said I couldn't
walk out here.

1362
01:03:45,455 --> 01:03:47,057
- [Speaking Russian]

1363
01:03:47,090 --> 01:03:48,893
- Begin? All right.
Thank you very much.

1364
01:03:48,927 --> 01:03:50,828
- They have Cole Porter there.

1365
01:03:51,962 --> 01:03:54,332
Is he supposed to be British?

1366
01:03:54,365 --> 01:03:57,502
- The power stations
and the controls are over there.

1367
01:03:57,535 --> 01:04:00,003
But they're heavily guarded.

1368
01:04:00,037 --> 01:04:01,338
I think
we'd have a better chance

1369
01:04:01,372 --> 01:04:03,573
of getting in the compound
from the other side.

1370
01:04:03,607 --> 01:04:07,212
- Be quiet. Shh.
You're a spy. Shh.

1371
01:04:10,113 --> 01:04:11,482
- Next.

1372
01:04:11,515 --> 01:04:13,985
- I was in Fantasticks
and Oklahoma.

1373
01:04:14,018 --> 01:04:15,887
I have a two-minute slide.

1374
01:04:15,920 --> 01:04:17,120
- Thank you, next.

1375
01:04:19,523 --> 01:04:22,360
- I was in The Odd Couple
and The Good Doctor.

1376
01:04:22,393 --> 01:04:24,495
I had a small part in
Leg's Diamond.

1377
01:04:24,528 --> 01:04:26,030
But then who didn't?

1378
01:04:26,063 --> 01:04:28,065
- Hold the rest.
I think we found our Gigi.

1379
01:04:28,098 --> 01:04:29,467
- Let me see your papers
authorizing you

1380
01:04:29,500 --> 01:04:31,302
to be in the area.

1381
01:04:31,335 --> 01:04:34,638
Well, actually all--

1382
01:04:34,672 --> 01:04:36,307
- Give me those.

1383
01:04:36,340 --> 01:04:39,343
- SAG After Equity.
Very impressive. You sing?

1384
01:04:39,377 --> 01:04:42,947
Hmm. Drive standard, automatic.
That's good.

1385
01:04:42,980 --> 01:04:47,250
- Mr. Steele, might I ask you
why you're in this district

1386
01:04:47,283 --> 01:04:49,220
when your papers clear you
only to Leningrad?

1387
01:04:49,253 --> 01:04:51,322
- May I ask you why you don't
have an accent?

1388
01:04:51,355 --> 01:04:54,292
- Actually,
I'm afraid I lost my way.

1389
01:04:54,325 --> 01:04:58,228
- I am not amused.
Do you realize the seriousness

1390
01:04:58,262 --> 01:05:00,430
of being found
on this military reservation?

1391
01:05:00,463 --> 01:05:03,200
- About as serious
as this guy's forehead.

1392
01:05:03,233 --> 01:05:06,370
- I'm sorry. In fact, if you
give me one of your chaps

1393
01:05:06,403 --> 01:05:08,205
to show me the way
back to the main road,

1394
01:05:08,238 --> 01:05:10,440
why, I'll be on my way
to Leningrad

1395
01:05:10,473 --> 01:05:11,508
and attend to my business.

1396
01:05:11,542 --> 01:05:13,010
- Perhaps you were spying on us.

1397
01:05:13,043 --> 01:05:14,312
- And you're smoking.

1398
01:05:14,345 --> 01:05:16,113
- Sabotage maybe.
Were you alone?

1399
01:05:16,147 --> 01:05:19,017
- Of course I was alone.

1400
01:05:19,050 --> 01:05:20,985
Look, nobody told me
at the embassy

1401
01:05:21,018 --> 01:05:23,553
that I couldn't go out and take
a bit of a walk in the woods.

1402
01:05:23,587 --> 01:05:25,555
I simply lost my way.

1403
01:05:25,588 --> 01:05:29,293
- You are lying. You will tell
me who was with you.

1404
01:05:29,327 --> 01:05:31,628
- I've told you before.
I was alone.

1405
01:05:31,662 --> 01:05:33,363
What else can I say to you?

1406
01:05:33,397 --> 01:05:36,100
- No who in their right minds
would go out with me?

1407
01:05:36,133 --> 01:05:38,535
- Good. Now let's do
some improv structure.

1408
01:05:41,672 --> 01:05:44,475
- I'll deal with you later.

1409
01:05:44,508 --> 01:05:47,210
- He's mad, don't you think?
You think he's mad?

1410
01:05:47,243 --> 01:05:48,578
I think--you know him.
Do you think he's mad?

1411
01:05:48,612 --> 01:05:50,447
- Rochester.

1412
01:05:50,480 --> 01:05:51,548
- I think he's mad.

1413
01:05:57,555 --> 01:05:59,423
[Imitating music]

1414
01:06:04,695 --> 01:06:07,130
- [Speaking Russian]

1415
01:06:14,037 --> 01:06:16,373
- The general is asking
for hamburgers

1416
01:06:16,407 --> 01:06:18,375
instead of chili peppers.
They burn his gut.

1417
01:06:18,408 --> 01:06:21,512
He'd really like a hamburger
and some French fried potatoes.

1418
01:06:23,280 --> 01:06:26,350
- [Imitates Russian general]

1419
01:06:26,383 --> 01:06:29,387
- The general
now calls us Spinach chins.

1420
01:06:29,420 --> 01:06:32,590
And they pause,
and they say nyet, nyet, nyet.

1421
01:06:35,458 --> 01:06:37,327
- The general scratches
his belly

1422
01:06:37,360 --> 01:06:40,765
and thinks his shirts are clean
but his officer stinks.

1423
01:06:40,798 --> 01:06:43,400
Gorilla girl hot and sweet.

1424
01:06:43,433 --> 01:06:46,737
A military matter.

1425
01:06:46,770 --> 01:06:49,040
No shaking the bush.

1426
01:06:50,473 --> 01:06:52,610
What's that story
about the bear again?

1427
01:06:55,745 --> 01:06:59,517
- Ha ha ha. Fun!

1428
01:06:59,550 --> 01:07:02,853
- You go first. After all,
you've taken the worst of it.

1429
01:07:06,723 --> 01:07:07,792
- [Sniffing]

1430
01:07:07,825 --> 01:07:09,260
- Hey.

1431
01:07:09,293 --> 01:07:10,762
See, that's what
I was talking about.

1432
01:07:10,795 --> 01:07:12,228
Right here. Commie scat.

1433
01:07:12,262 --> 01:07:14,565
- Piles of it everywhere.

1434
01:07:14,598 --> 01:07:16,500
- This is one
hell of a sand trap.

1435
01:07:16,533 --> 01:07:18,535
It's only a par three
on this thing too.

1436
01:07:18,568 --> 01:07:20,570
- Okay, careful.
Careful through here.

1437
01:07:20,603 --> 01:07:22,540
There you go.
Oh, that's a good girl.

1438
01:07:22,573 --> 01:07:24,742
There you go. Jump. Oh, you're
a good jumper, aren't you?

1439
01:07:24,775 --> 01:07:26,510
Yes, you are. Yes, you are.

1440
01:07:26,543 --> 01:07:30,815
That's so good. Very good.
Yes, yes, yes. Yes!

1441
01:07:30,848 --> 01:07:32,650
- Now limbo! How low can you go?

1442
01:07:32,683 --> 01:07:35,820
- Well, we already know
how low she can go.

1443
01:07:35,853 --> 01:07:37,822
- Ahem.

1444
01:07:37,855 --> 01:07:40,423
- Hmm. What's this?

1445
01:07:42,192 --> 01:07:45,095
Commie scat?

1446
01:07:45,128 --> 01:07:47,598
Hey, a woman was
humiliated here.

1447
01:07:47,632 --> 01:07:50,667
And then she was condescended to
and then someone used sarcasm.

1448
01:07:50,700 --> 01:07:52,168
- Somebody played right through
the sand trap

1449
01:07:52,202 --> 01:07:53,637
and didn't rake it.

1450
01:07:53,670 --> 01:07:56,107
That's the pits there,
isn't it?

1451
01:07:58,675 --> 01:08:03,413
- A lot of Ansel Adams-esque
scenery in this, isn't there?

1452
01:08:03,447 --> 01:08:06,550
Find your light.
Find your light.

1453
01:08:06,583 --> 01:08:09,787
- Hold it. Could somebody
get into the shot, please?

1454
01:08:09,820 --> 01:08:11,122
- Hey, anybody?

1455
01:08:11,155 --> 01:08:12,288
- Somebody on the screen,
please?

1456
01:08:12,322 --> 01:08:13,590
- Is somebody around
that could get--

1457
01:08:13,623 --> 01:08:16,327
- Oh, here he comes. Okay.
There you go.

1458
01:08:17,828 --> 01:08:19,397
- [Man yells in Russian,
gunfire]

1459
01:08:19,430 --> 01:08:21,332
- That must mean pull
in Russian.

1460
01:08:21,365 --> 01:08:24,202
- Oh, come on. Shake it off.
Suck it up. Get up and run.

1461
01:08:24,235 --> 01:08:26,303
Come on, you wuss. Get up.
Get up.

1462
01:08:26,337 --> 01:08:27,872
- She got the worst of it again.

1463
01:08:27,905 --> 01:08:29,907
- I'm all right.
- [Gunfire]

1464
01:08:29,940 --> 01:08:31,342
- Okay, I'm going. Hey ho.

1465
01:08:31,375 --> 01:08:33,210
Let me know which gulag
you're in.

1466
01:08:33,243 --> 01:08:35,178
- [Man yelling in Russian]

1467
01:08:35,212 --> 01:08:36,380
- If you lie very still

1468
01:08:36,413 --> 01:08:39,148
the Red bear will not step
on you.

1469
01:08:39,182 --> 01:08:43,353
- Now take my heat, Commie.

1470
01:08:43,387 --> 01:08:47,157
- Oh, my! I'd hate to shoot
a butt like that.

1471
01:08:47,190 --> 01:08:48,392
- Heh heh.

1472
01:08:52,830 --> 01:08:56,133
[Imitates music]

1473
01:08:58,468 --> 01:08:59,937
[Stops]

1474
01:08:59,970 --> 01:09:04,375
- [Imitating music]
- [Speaking Russian]

1475
01:09:04,408 --> 01:09:06,510
- Oh, what a day.

1476
01:09:06,543 --> 01:09:08,678
- Building a playground, huh?

1477
01:09:10,347 --> 01:09:12,717
- Just shaking the bosses, sir.

1478
01:09:12,750 --> 01:09:17,487
Bushes, I mean, Boss. Heh.

1479
01:09:17,520 --> 01:09:19,223
- There. They're building
a playground.

1480
01:09:19,257 --> 01:09:22,927
- It's really hardly guarded.
There's only one guy there.

1481
01:09:22,960 --> 01:09:25,862
He must be incredible
dependable.

1482
01:09:25,895 --> 01:09:27,665
I think I'll risk it.

1483
01:09:27,698 --> 01:09:30,733
- The grass is greener
on the Red side of the fence.

1484
01:09:30,767 --> 01:09:33,837
Trim Jeans Theater presents--

1485
01:09:33,870 --> 01:09:38,442
- Kitsch LeBeau,
can you hear me?

1486
01:09:41,712 --> 01:09:44,282
- If this follow spot
keeps up with me,

1487
01:09:44,315 --> 01:09:45,682
I'm gonna have to do a number.

1488
01:09:45,715 --> 01:09:49,253
Oh, let's see. One of my
Judy Garland favorites.

1489
01:09:49,287 --> 01:09:51,755
♪ By the light of-- ♪

1490
01:09:51,788 --> 01:09:57,962
I'm not doing two shows.
Definitely not doing two shows.

1491
01:09:57,995 --> 01:09:59,863
- Hmm. Look at this--
Fresh borscht.

1492
01:09:59,897 --> 01:10:01,632
We're going to have
to throw some of it out.

1493
01:10:01,665 --> 01:10:05,402
It's delicious. Nice sour cream
and beets. Mm-hmm.

1494
01:10:05,435 --> 01:10:08,772
- Come on. Come on. Ugh.

1495
01:10:08,805 --> 01:10:12,342
- Take my sister, will ya?
Tell your friend.

1496
01:10:12,375 --> 01:10:14,512
- That's it. One guard.

1497
01:10:14,545 --> 01:10:17,313
Most heavily guarded military
installation in all of Russia.

1498
01:10:17,347 --> 01:10:20,283
I'm in. Am I good or what?

1499
01:10:22,352 --> 01:10:24,822
- What's he going to do?
Ride it?

1500
01:10:24,855 --> 01:10:29,493
- This is going to get me
into Theta Chi for sure, dude.

1501
01:10:29,527 --> 01:10:33,263
- Hey, this is no time
for a snack. Come on.

1502
01:10:33,297 --> 01:10:35,598
- Can't vote, huh, Boris?

1503
01:10:35,632 --> 01:10:38,302
Well, this is how we vote
in the U.S. of A.

1504
01:10:38,335 --> 01:10:40,703
Take this you damn dirty
rat apes.

1505
01:10:40,737 --> 01:10:44,608
I'm taking a vote
for democracy and freedom.

1506
01:10:47,912 --> 01:10:49,547
- [Man speaking Russian]

1507
01:10:49,580 --> 01:10:51,415
- I'll just set this on brown.

1508
01:10:51,448 --> 01:10:53,783
- Yeah, three minutes
and leave it in the shell.

1509
01:10:53,817 --> 01:10:55,252
- That should do it.

1510
01:10:57,722 --> 01:11:00,523
And that's for making fun
of Nixon in the kitchen.

1511
01:11:00,557 --> 01:11:02,893
Here's a little present
for Mr. Stalin,

1512
01:11:02,927 --> 01:11:05,595
courtesy of Uncle Sam.

1513
01:11:05,628 --> 01:11:07,898
- That's for shaking your shoe
in the U.N.

1514
01:11:07,932 --> 01:11:10,533
- [Gunfire]

1515
01:11:10,567 --> 01:11:13,403
Now I escape
under the cover of darkness.

1516
01:11:14,405 --> 01:11:15,772
- [Man speaking Russian]

1517
01:11:15,805 --> 01:11:18,575
- How much are you
getting paid anyway?

1518
01:11:22,813 --> 01:11:24,080
- [Man speaking Russian]

1519
01:11:24,113 --> 01:11:28,052
- Clean, get over here,
you idiot.

1520
01:11:28,085 --> 01:11:29,387
- It's on here with Velcro.

1521
01:11:29,420 --> 01:11:30,653
Give me a hand.

1522
01:11:30,687 --> 01:11:32,522
- Uh, no, no, no.
Let me help you.

1523
01:11:32,555 --> 01:11:35,792
It's a child-proof bomb.
You have to push down and turn.

1524
01:11:35,825 --> 01:11:38,362
Go, this will look good
on your resume.

1525
01:11:41,698 --> 01:11:42,833
- Bye.

1526
01:11:44,402 --> 01:11:45,768
- Meep meep.

1527
01:11:47,437 --> 01:11:50,007
- Did I forget to tell you
to throw it?

1528
01:11:50,040 --> 01:11:53,343
- I'm all right. I'm okay.

1529
01:11:53,377 --> 01:11:56,913
- Hey, Tanya. Tanya, we did it!

1530
01:11:56,947 --> 01:11:58,515
Where are you, Tanya?

1531
01:11:59,517 --> 01:12:02,318
- Oh, there you are.

1532
01:12:04,955 --> 01:12:07,557
- What a dope

1533
01:12:07,590 --> 01:12:09,893
- Then Tanya and Bob
took up residence

1534
01:12:09,927 --> 01:12:11,962
with a man named Topper.

1535
01:12:11,995 --> 01:12:14,865
Hey, this doesn't
look like Russia.

1536
01:12:14,898 --> 01:12:17,600
Doesn't look like New York,
either.

1537
01:12:17,633 --> 01:12:20,537
Hazlet?
Must be a Twilight Zone episode.

1538
01:12:20,570 --> 01:12:23,540
- [Imitates Serling]
Submitted for your approval.

1539
01:12:23,573 --> 01:12:25,875
- Life was simple
in Grover's Corners.

1540
01:12:25,908 --> 01:12:29,045
- It's the new
1953 Buick Special.

1541
01:12:29,078 --> 01:12:30,847
And just like a dream,

1542
01:12:30,880 --> 01:12:33,617
you'll arrive
at the apocalypse in style.

1543
01:12:33,650 --> 01:12:35,852
- Special agents are called in
to slow the film down

1544
01:12:35,885 --> 01:12:37,855
and grind it
to a screeching halt.

1545
01:12:37,888 --> 01:12:40,457
- No one will be admitted
during the breathtaking

1546
01:12:40,490 --> 01:12:42,625
car parking sequence.

1547
01:12:44,628 --> 01:12:47,663
- What do we have to do
with this film, dear?

1548
01:12:47,697 --> 01:12:50,700
- Bill, I'm fighting.
Please don't go in today.

1549
01:12:50,733 --> 01:12:53,870
- Lots of men are staying home
until these air raids are over.

1550
01:12:53,903 --> 01:12:56,005
- I can't work over the phone
like they can. You know that.

1551
01:12:56,038 --> 01:12:58,575
Besides, I have to go.

1552
01:12:58,608 --> 01:13:00,043
- Just like you had
to go to Korea.

1553
01:13:00,077 --> 01:13:01,878
And before that, Formosa.

1554
01:13:01,912 --> 01:13:03,847
- And before that,
the Spanish-American War

1555
01:13:03,880 --> 01:13:05,648
and the Battle of 1812.

1556
01:13:05,682 --> 01:13:06,950
- You have to face
your responsibilities.

1557
01:13:06,983 --> 01:13:09,853
- That's what I'm doing.
Pat, in case of a raid,

1558
01:13:09,887 --> 01:13:11,655
there's nothing I can do
for you and Bill.

1559
01:13:11,688 --> 01:13:12,990
- You're going to have
to die on your own, dear.

1560
01:13:13,023 --> 01:13:14,925
- I can't take you by the hand
for everything.

1561
01:13:14,958 --> 01:13:17,962
- I can be of help on the air.
Pat, in a real emergency,

1562
01:13:17,995 --> 01:13:20,197
people will have to depend
on radio completely.

1563
01:13:20,230 --> 01:13:26,102
Besides, you can't believe all
those phony stories and rumors

1564
01:13:26,135 --> 01:13:28,105
the papers pick up and print.

1565
01:13:28,138 --> 01:13:30,840
- Maybe I'm silly.
- You are.

1566
01:13:30,873 --> 01:13:34,978
If anything should happen to
you, I don't know what I'd do.

1567
01:13:35,012 --> 01:13:38,182
- Don't worry.
Nothing's going to happen to me.

1568
01:13:38,215 --> 01:13:41,050
- You on the other hand,
you're meat.

1569
01:13:41,083 --> 01:13:43,487
Please, Pat, don't worry.

1570
01:13:43,520 --> 01:13:45,855
- Let's split, guys.

1571
01:13:45,888 --> 01:13:47,858
- See you later.

1572
01:13:47,892 --> 01:13:48,992
- Not.

1573
01:14:02,205 --> 01:14:04,107
- You know, I think you should
take it easy, Tom.

1574
01:14:04,140 --> 01:14:07,710
It looks cool. You kind of look
like Bart Simpson or something.

1575
01:14:07,743 --> 01:14:09,813
- Great.
- Uh oh, cool it you guys.

1576
01:14:09,847 --> 01:14:11,080
There's a foreign object
in our air space.

1577
01:14:11,113 --> 01:14:12,615
Cambot, give me give me rocket
number nine.

1578
01:14:12,648 --> 01:14:13,817
Let's have a look at it.

1579
01:14:13,850 --> 01:14:16,987
- We've got company, comrade.

1580
01:14:21,023 --> 01:14:26,095
- Greetings my new glasnost
comrade, I am Sori Adropoli--

1581
01:14:26,128 --> 01:14:27,898
most famous Soviet cosmonaut.

1582
01:14:27,932 --> 01:14:32,002
And these are my robot
help mates--Lucia and Vitalic.

1583
01:14:32,035 --> 01:14:36,773
I greet you in the name of all
working people of whole planet.

1584
01:14:36,807 --> 01:14:38,775
- What happened
to that guy's robots, Joel?

1585
01:14:38,808 --> 01:14:40,910
They look like they got
shot out of a canon.

1586
01:14:40,943 --> 01:14:43,747
- Be quiet. Hello.
I am Joel Robinson

1587
01:14:43,780 --> 01:14:45,115
of the satellite of love.

1588
01:14:45,148 --> 01:14:48,818
I greet you in the name
of all people of Earth

1589
01:14:48,852 --> 01:14:53,557
who are lounging around.
These are my robot companions--

1590
01:14:53,590 --> 01:14:55,992
Crow and Tom Servo.

1591
01:14:56,025 --> 01:15:00,197
- And we want to challenge
your robots to a game of hockey.

1592
01:15:00,230 --> 01:15:02,065
- Good one.

1593
01:15:02,098 --> 01:15:04,735
- It's very humorous.
It's most unusual for robot

1594
01:15:04,768 --> 01:15:06,102
to have sense of humor.

1595
01:15:06,135 --> 01:15:10,073
Let me show you one of
my good-natured mirth-getters.

1596
01:15:10,107 --> 01:15:11,875
I think this will
really get you. Hold on.

1597
01:15:11,908 --> 01:15:14,143
You know,
Sori not feeling so well.

1598
01:15:14,177 --> 01:15:17,580
He in unhappy mood.

1599
01:15:17,613 --> 01:15:21,818
Whoo! Bugga, bugga, bugga!
Sori, oh!

1600
01:15:21,852 --> 01:15:27,257
Oh, boy, I kill me. Ah.
Robots like it too.

1601
01:15:27,290 --> 01:15:32,128
Sorry. This is a minor
Soviet bypath malfunction.

1602
01:15:32,162 --> 01:15:35,032
Like in your country
is most important

1603
01:15:35,065 --> 01:15:37,567
to maintain mammoth
sense of humor,

1604
01:15:37,600 --> 01:15:39,870
and new wave American slapstick.

1605
01:15:39,903 --> 01:15:42,973
- I know why they call him Sori.
Geez.

1606
01:15:43,007 --> 01:15:47,043
- Um, hey, Sori, wait a minute.
Maybe we could do a link up.

1607
01:15:47,077 --> 01:15:50,113
I could fix your robot,
and then we could get together.

1608
01:15:50,147 --> 01:15:53,050
It's been so long since
I've seen another human being.

1609
01:15:53,083 --> 01:15:54,918
You know, we could connect
the access ports

1610
01:15:54,952 --> 01:15:57,620
and get together
and drink Tronya

1611
01:15:57,653 --> 01:15:59,188
or whatever it is
you guys do.

1612
01:15:59,222 --> 01:16:02,092
- Mr. Joel, I regret to inform
your misinformation.

1613
01:16:02,125 --> 01:16:06,128
But Soyet 18 is AC port.
And your ship is DC.

1614
01:16:06,162 --> 01:16:09,265
As we say in Soviet Union,
we are not compatible.

1615
01:16:09,298 --> 01:16:11,033
I'm so sorry. But I have to go.

1616
01:16:11,067 --> 01:16:13,970
- Hey, wait, wait.
Come back. Come back. Hold it.

1617
01:16:14,003 --> 01:16:15,038
- Yes?

1618
01:16:15,072 --> 01:16:16,707
- Is there anything
we could pass on

1619
01:16:16,740 --> 01:16:19,208
to our American brethren
on Earth?

1620
01:16:19,242 --> 01:16:21,378
- Yes, you could tell

1621
01:16:21,412 --> 01:16:24,313
your American glory vocalist
Billy Joel

1622
01:16:24,347 --> 01:16:26,650
that he did nothing
for glasnost.

1623
01:16:26,683 --> 01:16:28,018
And also we will trade you

1624
01:16:28,052 --> 01:16:31,153
a Billy Crystal
for a Yakov Smirnoff anytime.

1625
01:16:31,187 --> 01:16:33,190
So I got to go. Remember--

1626
01:16:33,223 --> 01:16:34,958
good health, self control,

1627
01:16:34,992 --> 01:16:37,827
and most important good mood.

1628
01:16:39,362 --> 01:16:41,365
- Do svidaniya.

1629
01:16:41,398 --> 01:16:44,167
- We have to remember
to pray for him.

1630
01:16:53,277 --> 01:16:56,347
- Well, looks like Italy.

1631
01:16:56,380 --> 01:16:58,982
- Hey, it's Metrano.

1632
01:16:59,015 --> 01:17:01,852
- [Humming]

1633
01:17:01,885 --> 01:17:04,988
- They're the chunkiest couple
on the block.

1634
01:17:05,022 --> 01:17:08,225
- Have a good day
at the log factory, dear.

1635
01:17:11,895 --> 01:17:14,230
- Honey, the steering wheel's
on the other side.

1636
01:17:14,263 --> 01:17:15,765
- If you want me to go out
with you tonight,

1637
01:17:15,798 --> 01:17:17,100
you've got to wear a tie.

1638
01:17:17,133 --> 01:17:19,668
I'm tired of everyone thinking
my husband's a bum.

1639
01:17:19,702 --> 01:17:22,872
- I am a bum
except for one thing.

1640
01:17:22,905 --> 01:17:24,407
- What's that?
- I'm a rich bum.

1641
01:17:24,440 --> 01:17:27,277
I had 20 bucks, and I'll blow it
all on you tonight.

1642
01:17:27,310 --> 01:17:30,247
- 20 bucks or not,
you're gonna wear a tie.

1643
01:17:30,280 --> 01:17:33,417
- Okay, I'll get a special one
just for you

1644
01:17:33,450 --> 01:17:35,452
with a new dame painted on it.

1645
01:17:35,485 --> 01:17:38,422
- Heh heh.
- Very funny.

1646
01:17:38,455 --> 01:17:40,757
- Very funny.
- See ya, sis.

1647
01:17:44,093 --> 01:17:45,828
- Well, let's swing it over
to New York

1648
01:17:45,862 --> 01:17:49,265
and see what's going on
in the saucy city. Heh.

1649
01:17:49,298 --> 01:17:52,802
Like it matters. Ugh.

1650
01:17:52,835 --> 01:17:55,105
- Uh, Mr. Sanford. Mr. San--

1651
01:17:55,138 --> 01:17:58,308
- Oh. Whew.
Almost a lawsuit there.

1652
01:17:58,342 --> 01:18:02,178
I'm Mr. Dirt. I come here
every day and watch the filth.

1653
01:18:02,212 --> 01:18:04,480
I'm a collector
of the world's refuse.

1654
01:18:04,513 --> 01:18:05,848
- Oh, man, this is definitely--

1655
01:18:05,882 --> 01:18:08,152
We've tapped
into a different movie here.

1656
01:18:08,185 --> 01:18:11,187
I could almost believe that last
scene with Art Metrano.

1657
01:18:11,220 --> 01:18:14,390
Oh, I don't understand this.
We've lost all common thread.

1658
01:18:14,423 --> 01:18:17,060
What is going on here?

1659
01:18:17,093 --> 01:18:18,728
- Hey, Crow,
something will happen soon.

1660
01:18:18,762 --> 01:18:20,330
- Good morning, my boy.

1661
01:18:20,363 --> 01:18:22,232
- Good morning, Mr. President.

1662
01:18:22,265 --> 01:18:25,235
- Well, I wanted to get
as far away from that plot

1663
01:18:25,268 --> 01:18:26,437
as I could get.

1664
01:18:26,470 --> 01:18:28,238
- Who's driving me bonkers?

1665
01:18:28,272 --> 01:18:30,340
- How's the market?
- Oh, Just fine.

1666
01:18:30,373 --> 01:18:32,075
With the war scare on,

1667
01:18:32,108 --> 01:18:34,177
you really should make
a killing.

1668
01:18:34,210 --> 01:18:38,215
- If there is a war, there will
be lots of killing, you guys.

1669
01:18:38,248 --> 01:18:40,350
- I'll just wait here.

1670
01:18:40,383 --> 01:18:43,085
- Good morning out there.
- Good morning in there.

1671
01:18:43,118 --> 01:18:45,955
- Bill Deal again with the news
and views of today.

1672
01:18:45,988 --> 01:18:48,492
And I must say for the first
time in a long time,

1673
01:18:48,525 --> 01:18:49,993
the outlook is brighter.

1674
01:18:50,027 --> 01:18:53,230
The Middle East and the Far East
are quiet.

1675
01:18:53,263 --> 01:18:54,197
At least temporarily.

1676
01:18:54,230 --> 01:18:55,965
And the Kremlin through
their foreign office

1677
01:18:55,998 --> 01:18:57,967
has made it known that
they're open for discussion

1678
01:18:58,000 --> 01:19:00,303
on matters that have long been
in disagreement.

1679
01:19:00,337 --> 01:19:03,973
Perhaps the large advances
we have recently made

1680
01:19:04,007 --> 01:19:06,477
in our missile program
has caused this new thinking

1681
01:19:06,510 --> 01:19:08,212
- on their part.
- Exclamation point.

1682
01:19:08,245 --> 01:19:10,380
- The successful launchings of
our mini satellites

1683
01:19:10,413 --> 01:19:12,248
apparently has closed the gap

1684
01:19:12,282 --> 01:19:14,017
in that missile race
that has existed

1685
01:19:14,050 --> 01:19:17,120
since they first launched
their Sputnik 1 many months ago.

1686
01:19:17,153 --> 01:19:21,492
Seem apparent that the new
powers in the Soviet Union

1687
01:19:21,525 --> 01:19:23,360
are far more interested in peace

1688
01:19:23,393 --> 01:19:26,563
and an ending to the Cold War
than their predecessors were.

1689
01:19:26,597 --> 01:19:29,398
It is reported that
a very important meeting

1690
01:19:29,432 --> 01:19:33,303
will take place in the Kremlin
at 8:30 pm Moscow time today.

1691
01:19:33,337 --> 01:19:36,372
That will be about three hours
from now.

1692
01:19:36,405 --> 01:19:39,408
So we'll try and have something
for you in the afternoon news.

1693
01:19:39,442 --> 01:19:41,277
Now for some local happenings...

1694
01:19:41,310 --> 01:19:44,413
The general is calling
a local tobacconist

1695
01:19:44,447 --> 01:19:47,350
to see if they have
Prince Albert in a can.

1696
01:19:47,383 --> 01:19:50,120
They say yes.
And the general says,

1697
01:19:50,153 --> 01:19:53,155
"Well you better let him out
before he suffocates."

1698
01:19:53,188 --> 01:19:56,225
And switches lines
to a local bowling alley.

1699
01:19:56,258 --> 01:19:59,828
And asks
if they have 10-pound balls.

1700
01:19:59,862 --> 01:20:02,132
They of course reply yes.
And he says,

1701
01:20:02,165 --> 01:20:07,337
"How did you get to the phone
so quickly?"

1702
01:20:09,305 --> 01:20:12,308
I thought that was funny,
didn't you?

1703
01:20:12,342 --> 01:20:14,443
- Very funny.

1704
01:20:14,477 --> 01:20:19,848
- Ha ha. What a joker, huh?

1705
01:20:19,882 --> 01:20:23,553
- 10 pound balls.
I can't believe it.

1706
01:20:23,587 --> 01:20:25,088
What a carrot boy.

1707
01:20:25,122 --> 01:20:27,057
- Somebody turned in
their shoes.

1708
01:20:27,090 --> 01:20:31,427
- Ahh.
Turn on lane number three here.

1709
01:20:33,062 --> 01:20:34,597
Ready on lane three.

1710
01:20:34,630 --> 01:20:38,435
- [Man speaking Russian]

1711
01:20:38,468 --> 01:20:40,537
[Beeping]

1712
01:20:40,570 --> 01:20:42,372
- Who's this artist?

1713
01:20:42,405 --> 01:20:45,075
- John Cage, I think.
- John Cage. Yeah, I got this.

1714
01:20:45,108 --> 01:20:47,977
- Wow. I finally get
my stereo tuned up

1715
01:20:48,010 --> 01:20:49,612
and it's this new wave crap.

1716
01:20:49,645 --> 01:20:52,182
If something better comes on
I can tape over this.

1717
01:20:52,215 --> 01:20:53,917
I can do it.

1718
01:20:53,950 --> 01:20:56,953
- [Man speaking Russian]

1719
01:20:56,987 --> 01:20:58,220
- Philip Glass.

1720
01:20:58,253 --> 01:21:02,192
- I think it's quadraphonic too.

1721
01:21:02,225 --> 01:21:04,293
- He's gassing up the rocket.

1722
01:21:05,928 --> 01:21:09,132
- We're filling the van
and heading to Steve's party.

1723
01:21:15,538 --> 01:21:17,107
- Go ahead number four.

1724
01:21:17,140 --> 01:21:19,675
Clean the windshield on that,
will ya.

1725
01:21:19,708 --> 01:21:23,513
- Wow. This is really dull.

1726
01:21:23,547 --> 01:21:27,883
- Hmm. Let's see if I can
tune in some light FM here.

1727
01:21:31,955 --> 01:21:35,425
Hmm. Back out to the playground.
Same songs.

1728
01:21:38,628 --> 01:21:41,530
- It just keeps doing that.

1729
01:21:41,563 --> 01:21:45,102
I never thought the end of
the world would be so annoying.

1730
01:21:45,135 --> 01:21:47,170
- Do not try to adjust
your sets.

1731
01:21:47,203 --> 01:21:49,005
We will control what is boring.

1732
01:21:49,038 --> 01:21:51,407
- This guy must be
at a self-service silo.

1733
01:21:51,440 --> 01:21:55,312
- Number four.
Gas is leaking at the 9:00 pm

1734
01:21:55,345 --> 01:21:59,115
- Four rubles back on a gas it
yourself with cash.

1735
01:21:59,148 --> 01:22:02,318
- [Man speaking Russian]

1736
01:22:05,722 --> 01:22:08,458
- Sure hope it doesn't go off
before the commercial.

1737
01:22:15,198 --> 01:22:16,565
[Beeping continues]

1738
01:22:16,598 --> 01:22:17,432
- Oh, great. More of this?

1739
01:22:17,465 --> 01:22:19,435
I think this stereo's
out of whack.

1740
01:22:19,468 --> 01:22:23,473
If he doesn't do the tape
monitor, he can--there we go.

1741
01:22:23,507 --> 01:22:26,743
- Oh, that rocket's run by
reel to reel.

1742
01:22:26,777 --> 01:22:30,247
It wasn't 'til '70s that
they were run by 8-track.

1743
01:22:30,280 --> 01:22:32,682
- Boy, that's annoying.

1744
01:22:32,715 --> 01:22:34,450
- Ah, cool!

1745
01:22:34,483 --> 01:22:39,522
- Really great
special effects here.

1746
01:22:39,555 --> 01:22:44,327
One .45 handgun.
Two packages of chewing gum.

1747
01:22:44,360 --> 01:22:47,463
One extra prophylactic.
One hundred--

1748
01:22:47,497 --> 01:22:50,267
- Okay, guys, at this point
we have to ask the question

1749
01:22:50,300 --> 01:22:52,302
what the hell did that
fat couple in the truck

1750
01:22:52,335 --> 01:22:54,237
have to do with anything here.

1751
01:22:56,538 --> 01:22:59,042
Hey, they're got more
of an annoying beep than us.

1752
01:22:59,075 --> 01:23:01,343
- And they got 80 geeks
at the controls.

1753
01:23:01,377 --> 01:23:02,712
- Hey, the invented asteroids.
Check it out.

1754
01:23:02,745 --> 01:23:04,980
- Oh, wow.

1755
01:23:09,318 --> 01:23:11,287
- Well, geez, look.
It's above my ears

1756
01:23:11,320 --> 01:23:13,390
and it's flat on top
and stuff.

1757
01:23:13,423 --> 01:23:16,558
And my scalp is bleeding
as it is.

1758
01:23:16,592 --> 01:23:19,695
- Look, I haven't got time
for the pain.

1759
01:23:19,728 --> 01:23:21,663
One moment please.

1760
01:23:21,697 --> 01:23:24,733
- Oh, it's one of your friends.
So called friends.

1761
01:23:24,767 --> 01:23:28,372
And he's really mean too.
I don't mind telling him.

1762
01:23:29,738 --> 01:23:33,242
- Watkins here. Yes.

1763
01:23:33,275 --> 01:23:35,545
Yes.

1764
01:23:35,578 --> 01:23:38,080
Are you sure it's not
an aircraft?

1765
01:23:38,113 --> 01:23:40,517
- Is it bigger than a bread box?

1766
01:23:40,550 --> 01:23:42,552
- Is it known for its work
in the theater?

1767
01:23:42,585 --> 01:23:44,553
- Have you got a plot
on its direction?

1768
01:23:44,587 --> 01:23:48,592
- So it's our eminent doom now.
Okay.

1769
01:23:48,625 --> 01:23:51,027
- Okay. Keep me informed.

1770
01:23:52,662 --> 01:23:54,197
Well, that's not good.

1771
01:23:56,298 --> 01:23:58,502
Defense man please.

1772
01:23:58,535 --> 01:24:01,703
Hello. Hello.
This is Gen. Watkins.

1773
01:24:01,737 --> 01:24:03,205
- No, it's not like the beer.

1774
01:24:03,238 --> 01:24:04,740
- Now, listen.

1775
01:24:04,773 --> 01:24:06,708
I want a red alert
for every area

1776
01:24:06,742 --> 01:24:08,612
east of the Mississippi River.

1777
01:24:08,645 --> 01:24:11,647
No further phone service
except for civil defense

1778
01:24:11,680 --> 01:24:13,717
and the military.

1779
01:24:13,750 --> 01:24:15,618
Put this on all teletypes--

1780
01:24:15,652 --> 01:24:19,555
At 14:00 hours Easter Standard
Time, the Reds fired a missile

1781
01:24:19,588 --> 01:24:23,560
aimed at the eastern seaboard
of the United States.

1782
01:24:23,593 --> 01:24:27,297
The missile will hit its target
in about 10 minutes.

1783
01:24:27,330 --> 01:24:28,698
No other facts are known.

1784
01:24:28,732 --> 01:24:31,533
But we'll try to keep you
informed.

1785
01:24:31,567 --> 01:24:35,738
- One more thing.
- Lunch next Wednesday? Okay.

1786
01:24:35,772 --> 01:24:39,575
- ...We're going to need them.

1787
01:24:39,608 --> 01:24:42,612
- I'm never going to finish
my novel now.

1788
01:24:42,645 --> 01:24:45,615
- Hey guys, look.
It's the beginning of Bonanza.

1789
01:24:45,648 --> 01:24:47,250
- Now we watch a the map burn
a whole in the middle,

1790
01:24:47,283 --> 01:24:48,818
and there come the Cartwrights.

1791
01:24:48,852 --> 01:24:50,787
- It went digital.

1792
01:24:50,820 --> 01:24:53,155
- [Air raid siren]

1793
01:24:53,188 --> 01:24:54,590
- Lunch time.
- Lunch!

1794
01:24:54,623 --> 01:25:00,128
- Hey, Queens. Queens, lunch.
- Break for lunch.

1795
01:25:00,162 --> 01:25:01,730
- Yonkers.

1796
01:25:01,763 --> 01:25:04,400
- Boy, this is no time to die.
I just bought a single slice.

1797
01:25:04,433 --> 01:25:08,805
- Attention, the remaining pizza
will go on sale for 15 cents.

1798
01:25:08,838 --> 01:25:09,605
- Hey, the cheese is cold.

1799
01:25:09,638 --> 01:25:13,408
Think you could nuke this
for me?

1800
01:25:15,378 --> 01:25:17,413
- That's not another
of those things.

1801
01:25:17,447 --> 01:25:19,348
- I think they're stupid.

1802
01:25:19,382 --> 01:25:22,685
- Darn old apocalypse.
I just ignore them.

1803
01:25:24,487 --> 01:25:28,223
- She says I'm a bum
because I don't wear a tie.

1804
01:25:28,257 --> 01:25:33,495
I went out and bought it. Man
alive. She's gonna love this.

1805
01:25:33,528 --> 01:25:36,732
- Come on, you guys.
- Quit horsing around.

1806
01:25:36,765 --> 01:25:38,467
Give me that.

1807
01:25:38,500 --> 01:25:40,270
This one here's too much.

1808
01:25:40,303 --> 01:25:41,872
Yeah, you're high.

1809
01:25:41,905 --> 01:25:44,607
- Wow.
- Hear the pretty music.

1810
01:25:47,377 --> 01:25:48,745
-Hey, it's Klitch Cago.

1811
01:25:48,778 --> 01:25:51,480
- Attention everyone,
this is a red alert.

1812
01:25:51,513 --> 01:25:55,252
- It's Harry Connick Jr.
Red Alert.

1813
01:25:55,285 --> 01:25:58,822
Benny Goodman in Moscow. Groovy.

1814
01:25:58,855 --> 01:25:59,955
- ...switch to civil defense.

1815
01:25:59,988 --> 01:26:01,457
- Remember that good stuff
I told you about earlier,

1816
01:26:01,490 --> 01:26:04,393
well forget it.
And now simply red.

1817
01:26:04,427 --> 01:26:07,330
- Repeat, switch to 640 or 1240
on your dial

1818
01:26:07,363 --> 01:26:09,398
and get your instructions
from civilian defense.

1819
01:26:09,432 --> 01:26:11,733
This is a raid. Take shelter.

1820
01:26:11,767 --> 01:26:13,870
- Remember to pick up a few
KQ bumper stickers.

1821
01:26:13,903 --> 01:26:16,605
- Please keep the lines free
for military calls only.

1822
01:26:16,638 --> 01:26:18,942
- You'll electrocute me
if you pick up the phone.

1823
01:26:18,975 --> 01:26:20,208
No really,
I'm working on the line.

1824
01:26:20,242 --> 01:26:24,480
- Listen on 640 or 1240 on your
dial for further instructions.

1825
01:26:24,513 --> 01:26:26,515
- Oh, and honey, could you turn
the pilot light off?

1826
01:26:26,548 --> 01:26:27,983
- Attention,
this is a real raid.

1827
01:26:28,017 --> 01:26:29,952
- Hey, it's Toody and Muldoon.

1828
01:26:29,985 --> 01:26:31,720
And they got Glenn Super
in the car.

1829
01:26:31,753 --> 01:26:35,692
- How would you like
to use this in a supermarket?

1830
01:26:35,725 --> 01:26:37,960
- Supermarket.
- Attention, attention.

1831
01:26:37,993 --> 01:26:43,365
- Even little children must die
so the lesson must be learned.

1832
01:26:46,468 --> 01:26:49,272
- Snap.
- Oh, my neck.

1833
01:26:50,473 --> 01:26:55,478
- Even sister Sue must die
in a grisly death.

1834
01:26:58,013 --> 01:27:02,518
Even Rod Steiger must die.

1835
01:27:04,287 --> 01:27:05,388
- Careful there.

1836
01:27:05,422 --> 01:27:08,323
- Oh, my head. Ouch.

1837
01:27:08,357 --> 01:27:09,792
- Hey,
where'd you get your pants?

1838
01:27:09,825 --> 01:27:13,462
Oh, that's right,
same place as me.

1839
01:27:13,495 --> 01:27:14,997
I object to this, you know.

1840
01:27:15,030 --> 01:27:16,865
- To those of you still
listening,

1841
01:27:16,898 --> 01:27:19,735
follow this instructions
if you can't get underground.

1842
01:27:19,768 --> 01:27:21,737
- Try not to let
your hair catch on fire.

1843
01:27:21,770 --> 01:27:24,507
- ...lie still
until the blast has subsided.

1844
01:27:24,540 --> 01:27:26,742
Don't touch or eat anything.
And above all,

1845
01:27:26,775 --> 01:27:28,778
keep calm and follow
instructions.

1846
01:27:28,812 --> 01:27:30,278
- For a hundred years.
- Drink lots of juice.

1847
01:27:30,312 --> 01:27:31,982
It's good for you.
Or have an orange.

1848
01:27:32,015 --> 01:27:33,850
- ...and 1240 on your dial.

1849
01:27:33,883 --> 01:27:35,652
- Now back to Klitch Cargo.

1850
01:27:37,553 --> 01:27:40,022
- Okay, let me have it.
- Okay, you dress bad

1851
01:27:40,055 --> 01:27:43,727
- and your suit's ugly.
- That's what I thought, yes.

1852
01:27:43,760 --> 01:27:46,928
- You want me to hold
the chicken?

1853
01:27:46,962 --> 01:27:51,833
Do you just want wheat toast?
Oh, that's a number 13. Sure.

1854
01:27:51,867 --> 01:27:56,705
- Bob, Jerry's waiting for you
in his office.

1855
01:27:56,738 --> 01:28:01,877
- ...This thing is coming
straight down.

1856
01:28:01,910 --> 01:28:04,547
There's a slim chance.

1857
01:28:04,580 --> 01:28:05,648
- One delicious shake
in the morning

1858
01:28:05,682 --> 01:28:07,583
and my hair falls out
in the evening.

1859
01:28:07,617 --> 01:28:08,818
- Mack at controls please.

1860
01:28:11,820 --> 01:28:15,492
This is Gen. Watkins here.
Yes. Yes.

1861
01:28:15,525 --> 01:28:18,393
- Yeah, like the beer.

1862
01:28:18,427 --> 01:28:19,828
- When this missile shows
on your scopes,

1863
01:28:19,862 --> 01:28:22,532
I want you to fire
every Nike you have.

1864
01:28:22,565 --> 01:28:25,502
- Yeah, and Adidas too.
Tretorns, Keds, Red Ball Jets.

1865
01:28:25,535 --> 01:28:27,337
The whole shebang.

1866
01:28:27,970 --> 01:28:29,805
- But try.

1867
01:28:29,838 --> 01:28:34,677
- Please try.
- Okay.

1868
01:28:34,710 --> 01:28:38,513
- If only we had and operational
anti-missile device.

1869
01:28:38,547 --> 01:28:42,418
- Say, my uncle Louie
has one in the basement.

1870
01:28:42,452 --> 01:28:44,787
- Maybe the Nike will hit.

1871
01:28:44,820 --> 01:28:47,123
- Maybe the Nike will hit.
Nyeh. Nyeh.

1872
01:28:47,157 --> 01:28:48,590
It's just something to do.

1873
01:28:48,623 --> 01:28:50,560
- ...18,000 miles and hour.

1874
01:28:50,593 --> 01:28:52,528
Nothing can stop it.

1875
01:28:52,562 --> 01:28:53,763
- [Alarm buzzes repeatedly]

1876
01:28:53,797 --> 01:28:56,632
- Excuse me. Excuse me.
Excuse me. Excuse me

1877
01:28:56,665 --> 01:28:58,968
- Chili.

1878
01:28:59,002 --> 01:29:02,805
- Kids, remember,
don't lay in refrigerators.

1879
01:29:02,838 --> 01:29:05,408
They don't open
from the other side.

1880
01:29:05,442 --> 01:29:09,645
[Buzzer continues]

1881
01:29:09,678 --> 01:29:11,847
- Good morning.
- Hit the snooze.

1882
01:29:11,880 --> 01:29:13,415
- Somebody please slap
the snooze.

1883
01:29:13,448 --> 01:29:15,452
- Morning everyone.

1884
01:29:15,485 --> 01:29:17,487
You get like this
in the morning.

1885
01:29:17,520 --> 01:29:18,955
- Oh, yeah.
- Me, too.

1886
01:29:18,988 --> 01:29:22,392
- Hey, look, a trident.

1887
01:29:22,425 --> 01:29:24,760
- Sugar free?

1888
01:29:26,095 --> 01:29:29,132
That's a Nike and a Ked.

1889
01:29:29,165 --> 01:29:32,602
That's a Tretorn.

1890
01:29:32,635 --> 01:29:34,037
- Calling all kids.

1891
01:29:34,070 --> 01:29:36,705
- Air Jordan. There it does.

1892
01:29:40,208 --> 01:29:42,812
- That one was an oxford.

1893
01:29:42,845 --> 01:29:45,882
- Shaped like
a saber tooth tiger.

1894
01:29:45,915 --> 01:29:48,952
- Taxi.
- Those are missiles not taxis.

1895
01:29:48,985 --> 01:29:52,122
- Hey, it's Harry Truman.

1896
01:29:52,155 --> 01:29:54,490
- Got to get out of here.

1897
01:29:54,523 --> 01:29:57,760
- I got to go.

1898
01:29:57,793 --> 01:30:00,195
- Got to go.

1899
01:30:00,228 --> 01:30:02,532
- Getting out of the city.

1900
01:30:04,000 --> 01:30:08,103
- Hey, it's a special post-
apocalyptic collector's edition.

1901
01:30:08,137 --> 01:30:13,142
- Attention Zack Norman is dead.
- Stop the presses.

1902
01:30:13,175 --> 01:30:14,843
Heh. It worked.

1903
01:30:14,877 --> 01:30:17,980
Heh, lucky one.
Didn't have to see any of this.

1904
01:30:24,520 --> 01:30:29,792
- Help me.
- What?

1905
01:30:29,825 --> 01:30:33,997
- I know which shelter
I'm going to. Yeah.

1906
01:30:34,030 --> 01:30:37,167
- [Imitates Dr. Strangelove]
Of course women must be chosen

1907
01:30:37,200 --> 01:30:38,868
for their sexual prowess.

1908
01:30:38,902 --> 01:30:41,870
Und animals will be
bred and slaughtered.

1909
01:30:41,903 --> 01:30:44,540
- ...have to discuss retaliation
for this unwarranted attack.

1910
01:30:44,573 --> 01:30:46,608
- Oh, yeah. Like that's
really going to help.

1911
01:30:46,642 --> 01:30:47,943
- ...move to its emergency
headquarters,

1912
01:30:47,977 --> 01:30:50,513
and all military personnel
are asked to report

1913
01:30:50,547 --> 01:30:52,648
to their bases as soon as
they safely can.

1914
01:30:52,682 --> 01:30:55,017
- Like in about 5,000 years.

1915
01:30:57,987 --> 01:31:01,257
- But in the meantime, enjoy
Jerry Lewis and Tony Curtis

1916
01:31:01,290 --> 01:31:03,758
in Boeing, Boeing!

1917
01:31:10,633 --> 01:31:12,902
Boy, that is one long runway.

1918
01:31:12,935 --> 01:31:15,538
That plane just can't seem
to get off the ground.

1919
01:31:15,572 --> 01:31:17,907
- Mm-hmm.

1920
01:31:17,940 --> 01:31:21,810
- Starting in airport 79.
Damn you all to hell!

1921
01:31:21,843 --> 01:31:26,115
- George Kennedy pops the clutch
and tells the world eat my dust.

1922
01:31:26,148 --> 01:31:29,518
- Clutch.

1923
01:31:29,552 --> 01:31:31,087
[Air raid siren blares]

1924
01:31:31,120 --> 01:31:34,057
- There's a severe storm warning
for Wright and Jackson county

1925
01:31:34,090 --> 01:31:35,658
and parts of Carver county.

1926
01:31:35,692 --> 01:31:38,627
All New York
is in this warning area.

1927
01:31:38,660 --> 01:31:41,763
- Damn!
- The teletypes are dead.

1928
01:31:41,797 --> 01:31:43,865
- The only people that
are left are Harry Belafonte,

1929
01:31:43,898 --> 01:31:45,702
Anger Stevens
and that other guy.

1930
01:31:45,735 --> 01:31:48,303
This is Bill Deal signing off.

1931
01:31:48,337 --> 01:31:51,840
- And remember Bill Deal's
Oldsmobile at at the beltway.

1932
01:31:51,873 --> 01:31:53,808
- Dear, if you can hear me,

1933
01:31:53,842 --> 01:31:57,145
it just dawned on me
that I probably won't see you

1934
01:31:57,178 --> 01:31:59,182
or little Bill again.

1935
01:31:59,215 --> 01:32:03,152
This is because I've been
stubborn. I'm sorry.

1936
01:32:03,185 --> 01:32:05,622
'Cause I really love you.

1937
01:32:05,655 --> 01:32:08,925
- What a wuss.
- [Phone rings]

1938
01:32:08,958 --> 01:32:11,193
- Nice pirouette.

1939
01:32:11,227 --> 01:32:13,262
- Gen. Watkins.

1940
01:32:13,295 --> 01:32:14,763
- So I call and I get Fiji.

1941
01:32:14,797 --> 01:32:16,932
And I say AT&T
would've given me credit.

1942
01:32:16,965 --> 01:32:18,200
They say you're not dealing
with AT&T.

1943
01:32:18,233 --> 01:32:20,168
Say I am now.

1944
01:32:20,202 --> 01:32:23,705
- Well, at least I don't have
a lot of hair to burn.

1945
01:32:25,207 --> 01:32:27,643
- Darn.

1946
01:32:27,677 --> 01:32:31,147
- Give me the White House.
- I quit.

1947
01:32:31,180 --> 01:32:34,750
- Clutch!

1948
01:32:40,823 --> 01:32:44,227
- Turned the Big Apple
into apple sauce.

1949
01:32:44,260 --> 01:32:45,995
- The suburbs were spared?

1950
01:32:46,028 --> 01:32:47,897
- ...New York City was almost
completely destroyed

1951
01:32:47,930 --> 01:32:51,100
with a death toll of well over
three million.

1952
01:32:51,133 --> 01:32:52,935
- Just a regular day
in the Big Apple.

1953
01:32:52,968 --> 01:32:56,638
- Regardless of possible
disagreements--

1954
01:32:56,672 --> 01:32:58,673
- Really a fun film guys.

1955
01:32:58,707 --> 01:33:02,177
- Let's beat it.

1956
01:33:02,210 --> 01:33:05,380
- We cannot let this be the end.

1957
01:33:05,413 --> 01:33:07,650
- Oh, yes we can.

1958
01:33:22,263 --> 01:33:27,202
[Crosstalk]

1959
01:33:30,907 --> 01:33:33,808
- Now, now, now.
You're all speaking at once.

1960
01:33:33,842 --> 01:33:36,845
And I can't understand you.
You're upset.

1961
01:33:36,878 --> 01:33:38,813
Please, one at a time.

1962
01:33:38,847 --> 01:33:41,717
- Okay, okay, guys,
let's jus take it easy.

1963
01:33:41,750 --> 01:33:45,120
[Crosstalk]

1964
01:33:50,125 --> 01:33:51,192
- Hold it. Hold it.

1965
01:33:51,225 --> 01:33:54,997
Listen, maybe we should just
take this one at a time

1966
01:33:55,030 --> 01:33:57,332
and try to explain
how we're feeling, okay?

1967
01:33:57,365 --> 01:33:58,432
Okay, go ahead, Tom.
You start.

1968
01:33:58,465 --> 01:34:01,003
- Well, first of all,
Sam Waterson sends a spy guy

1969
01:34:01,037 --> 01:34:02,303
in the Piper Cub over to Russia

1970
01:34:02,337 --> 01:34:03,772
to a restaurant
with bad service.

1971
01:34:03,805 --> 01:34:05,808
And then he meets the girl
who dates a pig.

1972
01:34:05,842 --> 01:34:06,942
And he lives in her closet
for 10 year

1973
01:34:06,975 --> 01:34:09,145
while they develop the
United States rocket program.

1974
01:34:09,178 --> 01:34:11,247
- Yeah, yeah.
And the relationship

1975
01:34:11,280 --> 01:34:13,215
between the spy girl
and the fat, stinky,

1976
01:34:13,248 --> 01:34:16,785
balding Russian pig guy only
gets better, that's disgusting.

1977
01:34:16,818 --> 01:34:19,955
- Enter Leonard Nimoy,
Bill Bixby type.

1978
01:34:19,988 --> 01:34:23,225
He hands 'em a megaton of TNT,
leads them into the woods,

1979
01:34:23,258 --> 01:34:25,995
and suddenly they're having
a teary departure

1980
01:34:26,028 --> 01:34:27,262
like we're supposed to care.

1981
01:34:27,295 --> 01:34:30,165
I mean, we didn't even know the
guy was supposed to be a Brit.

1982
01:34:30,198 --> 01:34:34,470
All he did was say things like
cheerio and good boy.

1983
01:34:34,503 --> 01:34:37,138
- Then sui the pig girl
gets shot

1984
01:34:37,172 --> 01:34:39,742
in one of the least dramatic
scenes since Date with an Angel.

1985
01:34:39,775 --> 01:34:41,343
Spy guy muffs the bombing
on the missile

1986
01:34:41,377 --> 01:34:42,577
guarded by the photo met.

1987
01:34:42,610 --> 01:34:45,047
And then he gets shot in the
same place as sui the pig girl!

1988
01:34:45,080 --> 01:34:47,717
- Oh, cut to New York,
where Art Metrano

1989
01:34:47,750 --> 01:34:49,452
and Harry Connick eat pizza
and buy ties

1990
01:34:49,485 --> 01:34:51,153
for their stupid girlfriends.

1991
01:34:51,187 --> 01:34:52,888
And Harry Truman gets off
a plane by the sewage dump.

1992
01:34:52,922 --> 01:34:55,190
And then a blind guy goes by
and says, "Help me."

1993
01:34:55,223 --> 01:34:56,792
What the hell was that
supposed to be?

1994
01:34:56,825 --> 01:34:58,227
- Yeah. And then there's
the glorious ending

1995
01:34:58,260 --> 01:35:02,197
where the entire center
of New York City explodes.

1996
01:35:02,230 --> 01:35:04,100
Except for the perimeters.
And all we learn

1997
01:35:04,133 --> 01:35:07,335
is that we're supposed to live
in the suburbs not in the city.

1998
01:35:07,368 --> 01:35:11,107
I got to read some letters.

1999
01:35:11,140 --> 01:35:14,910
Okay. Put this one up
on the screen there, Cambot.

2000
01:35:14,943 --> 01:35:16,478
Still store it for me.

2001
01:35:16,512 --> 01:35:22,083
"The main reason for this letter
is to call to your attention

2002
01:35:22,117 --> 01:35:25,220
"a matter of grave importance.
I was visiting a friend's house

2003
01:35:25,253 --> 01:35:28,357
"when I noticed his childhood
stuffed bunny rabbit.

2004
01:35:28,390 --> 01:35:30,092
"What I found was horrible.

2005
01:35:30,125 --> 01:35:32,495
See the enclosed,
un-retouched photograph."

2006
01:35:32,528 --> 01:35:34,497
Okay, put that up there, Cambot.

2007
01:35:34,530 --> 01:35:36,865
Okay, there's the bunny
right there.

2008
01:35:36,898 --> 01:35:39,802
"This poor bunny
had been designed

2009
01:35:39,835 --> 01:35:42,203
"to look like Isaac Asimov.

2010
01:35:42,237 --> 01:35:45,140
"What kind of cruel, sadistic
world do we live in

2011
01:35:45,173 --> 01:35:47,375
where injustices such as these
are allowed to happen?"

2012
01:35:47,408 --> 01:35:48,375
- Bet he was a scientist.

2013
01:35:48,408 --> 01:35:51,380
- Yeah, could've had something
to do with that.

2014
01:35:51,413 --> 01:35:53,482
Well, what do you think of this
week's experiment, you guys?

2015
01:35:53,515 --> 01:35:54,582
As if I care.

2016
01:35:54,615 --> 01:35:59,522
- You're upset. I like that.
Push the button, Frank.

2017
01:35:59,555 --> 01:36:01,290
- It was kind of harsh.

2018
01:36:01,323 --> 01:36:02,992
I mean, why don't you give him
a break next time.

2019
01:36:03,025 --> 01:36:05,093
Like show him Scott Valentine's
My Demon Lover

2020
01:36:05,127 --> 01:36:07,395
or Betsy's Wedding
or better yet--

2021
01:36:07,428 --> 01:36:08,463
- Push the button.

2022
01:36:08,497 --> 01:36:10,265
- Madame Sousatzka.

2023
01:36:10,298 --> 01:36:13,102
- Push the button, Frank.
- Madame Sousatzka.

2024
01:36:13,135 --> 01:36:15,972
- Have you seen that?
It's actually very good.

2025
01:36:16,005 --> 01:36:17,405
- Is it?
- With Shirley MacLaine.

2026
01:36:17,438 --> 01:36:20,142
She does a wonderful job.

2027
01:36:20,175 --> 01:36:22,110
- Let's go catch it. Okay.

2028
01:37:33,248 --> 01:37:34,850
Help me.

